/**
 * brands, long-form SEO overrides
 */
module.exports = {
  "myfather": "<p><strong>My Father Cigars</strong> is the García family company. <a href=\"/node/pepin/\">Pepín García</a> is the patriarch. His son <a href=\"/node/jaimegarcia/\">Jaime García</a> blended the namesake My Father cigar in 2008 as a tribute, and the Estelí factory that opened in 2009 took that name. Flagship lines include My Father, The Judge (Cigar of the Year 2024), Le Bijou 1922 (Cigar of the Year 2015), Flor de las Antillas (Cigar of the Year 2012), and La Opulencia. The style is full Nicaraguan flavor with Cuban-inspired construction.</p>\n<p>The factory also rolls for other brands: <a href=\"/node/tatuaje/\">Tatuaje</a>, <a href=\"/node/latelier/\">L'Atelier</a>, <a href=\"/node/crownedheads/\">Crowned Heads</a>, and Ashton Nicaraguan marques (<a href=\"/node/laaromadecuba/\">La Aroma de Cuba</a>, <a href=\"/node/sancristobal/\">San Cristóbal</a>). That dual identity (own marca plus contract floor) places My Father next to <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernandez</a> and <a href=\"/node/plasencia/\">Plasencia</a> as infrastructure, not only a consumer brand. Family-owned. Not STG.</p>",
  "tatuaje": "<p><strong>Tatuaje</strong> (Spanish for tattoo) is the boutique brand <a href=\"/node/petejohnson/\">Pete Johnson</a> launched in <strong>2003</strong>. Johnson came out of Los Angeles cigar retail and named the marca for his tattoos. That year he met <a href=\"/node/pepin/\">Pepín García</a>, who rolled the first Tatuajes at <a href=\"/node/elreyhabanos/\">El Rey de los Habanos</a> in Miami's Little Havana. Tatuaje was Pepín's first private-label client, and the cigars helped introduce García Cuban-style construction to a new wave of U.S. smokers. Who makes Tatuaje? The García family does. Pete owns the brand. Pepín, Jaime, and the factory roll it.</p>\n<p>When the Garcías built <a href=\"/node/myfather/\">My Father</a> in Estelí, Tatuaje moved with them. Core lines include Brown Label (Selección de Cazador), Black Label, Havana VI, Fausto, Cojonú, and Reserva, plus years of limiteds that taught boutique smokers to hunt boxes. The style is Cuban-inspired: triple cap, slide-lid cabinets, full Nicaraguan flavor. Johnson's later sister marca, <a href=\"/node/latelier/\">L'Atelier</a> (2012), is a separate catalog (Sancti Spíritus leaf, wine-world names), also made with the Garcías. Tatuaje is the tattoo-shop brand. L'Atelier is the workshop brand. Neither is an STG or Altadis name.</p>\n<p>The García family has rolled every Tatuaje, including the Honduran Series P made at My Father's Honduras factory. The through-line does not change. If you are asking where Tatuaje is made, start at El Rey for the origin story and My Father in Estelí for the cigars on shelves today. Crowned Heads and other García clients share that factory culture. Tatuaje is still Pete's marca on García manufacturing.</p>",
  "padron": "<p><strong>Padrón</strong> is one of the most respected names in premium cigars: a family-owned Nicaraguan house founded in <strong>1964</strong> in Miami by Cuban émigré <a href=\"/node/joseopadron/\">José Orlando Padrón</a>. After the Revolution ended the family's Pinar del Río tobacco life, Orlando started with a handful of rollers and a hammer he kept as a reminder of hard work, then bet early on Nicaraguan leaf. By <strong>1970</strong> production had moved to Estelí at what is now <a href=\"/node/tabacoscubanica/\">Tabacos Cubanica</a>. The company survived civil war, factory disasters, and embargo-era chaos without abandoning its model: grow and age its own tobacco, refuse flash marketing, sell through tight retailer relationships, and let consistency do the talking.</p>\n<p>The brand ladder is simple and famous. The numbered regular line (2000 through 7000 and related sizes) is the everyday Padrón puro. <a href=\"/node/padron1964/\">1964 Anniversary</a> marks the founding year with multi-year aged leaf and box-pressed elegance. <a href=\"/node/padron1926/\">Serie 1926</a> honors Orlando's birth year at the top of the pyramid. Natural and Maduro wrappers run across the range; almost everything is a Nicaraguan puro built from family inventory that often ages for years before it is rolled, then ages again as finished cigars. Leadership today includes <a href=\"/node/jorgepadron/\">Jorge Padrón</a> (president) and family partners such as <a href=\"/node/orlandopadron/\">Orlando Padrón</a>, continuing the no-sales-force culture José Orlando set until his death in 2017.</p>\n<p>On Cigar Nexus, Padrón is the benchmark Nicaraguan family puro node. Cross-link to Tabacos Cubanica for the factory, to 1964 and 1926 for the prestige series, and to José Orlando, Jorge, and Orlando for the people graph. Searchers comparing Padrón to Fuente, Oliva, or My Father need this ownership and aging story more than tasting notes alone.</p>",
  "rojas": "<p><strong>Rojas Cigars</strong> is the boutique brand founded by <a href=\"/node/noelrojas/\">Noel Rojas</a>. Lines include <a href=\"/node/guayacan/\">Guayacan</a>, Unfinished Business, Street Tacos, KSG, and Quiet Morning. KSG stands for \"King of Small Gauge,\" Rojas's nickname for himself, and it reflects his focus on thinner vitolas. The first KSG, a 7 1/2 x 38 lancero, debuted in April 2020 with tobacco Rojas bought in 2019 from Somoto, a lesser-known region near the Honduran border. A 6 1/2 x 42 KSG Limited Edition 2026 is due in November 2026.</p>\n<p>The Street Tacos series, inspired by Mexican street food, debuted in November 2020. It has since added an annual Cinco de Mayo limited edition (2024, 2025, and 2026), Al Pastor (2025), and Mictlán, a 2026 Día de los Muertos release. One early collaboration, the Street Tacos Elote Firecracker made for United Cigars, was rolled at Tabacalera Flor de San Luis in Nicaragua. Quiet Morning, a regular-production line pitched as the first cigar of the day, arrived at PCA 2026.</p>\n<p>Current Rojas cigars are made at the <a href=\"/node/rojasfactory/\">Rojas Cigar Factory</a>, a converted movie theater in Estelí. Rojas says all the filler and binder comes from his own farms in Condega and Jalapa. The same factory makes <a href=\"/node/stolenthrone/\">Stolen Throne</a>, and earlier Rojas lines came from <a href=\"/node/neworder/\">New Order of the Ages</a> (2016–18).</p>",
  "guayacan": "<p><strong>Guayacan</strong> is the brand that introduced <a href=\"/node/noelrojas/\">Noel Rojas</a> to the U.S. market. When he first showed it at the 2013 IPCPR show, Rojas owned and ran his own Estelí factory, Tabacalera Aromas de Jalapa. After the partners there split in late 2014, Rojas and Renegade Cigars owner Brandon Hayes opened New Order of the Ages (NOA), a 5,000-square-foot Estelí factory, on January 1, 2016. Guayacan moved there, and NOA also made boutique brands such as Ezra Zion, Que Vida, and <a href=\"/node/jassumkral/\">Jas Sum Kral</a>.</p>\n<p>Guayacan Rojas Reserve arrived in 2015, followed by a Connecticut version. The line now sits in the <a href=\"/node/rojas/\">Rojas Cigars</a> portfolio. Rojas later converted an old Estelí movie theater into the <a href=\"/node/rojasfactory/\">Rojas Cigar Factory</a>.</p>\n<p>Earlier versions of this map tied Guayacan to A.J. Fernandez, San Lotano, and My Father. We found no source for any of them, so those links have been removed.</p>",
  "warped": "<p><strong>Warped Cigars</strong>, founded by <a href=\"/node/kylegellis/\">Kyle Gellis</a>, is one of the most respected boutique brands of the 2010s–2020s: traditional construction, deep tobacco nerd energy, and a preference for expressive Nicaraguan and other Central American leaf over gimmicks. Lines such as Corto, Sarto, Cloud Hopper, Futuro, La Hacienda, Guardian of the Farm collaborations, and El Oso built a following that treats Warped as a connoisseur brand rather than a hype-drop machine, even when releases are small.</p>\n<p>Production history tracks the Aganorsa-to-NACSA boutique pipeline. Early and ongoing relationships with <a href=\"/node/tabsa/\">TABSA</a> / <a href=\"/node/aganorsa/\">Aganorsa</a> put Warped in the same tobacco ecosystem as <a href=\"/node/illusione/\">Illusione</a> and <a href=\"/node/viaje/\">Viaje</a>. More recent major work at <a href=\"/node/nacsa/\">NACSA</a> in Estelí (and occasional Joya projects) reflects Gellis's hands-on factory oversight. The brand's \"old world in a new world factory\" positioning is deliberate: sun-grown character, classic vitolas, and packaging that signals serious smoke rather than lifestyle merch.</p>\n<p>Map Warped as founder-led U.S. boutique with Estelí contract homes at NACSA/TABSA. Pair with Kyle Gellis, Illusione, and Viaje for the Aganorsa-era peer set, and with Crowned Heads/Foundation when contrasting My Father vs Aganorsa factory cultures inside the same boutique generation.</p>",
  "joya": "<p><strong>Joya de Nicaragua</strong> is the country's historic national cigar brand, founded in <strong>1968</strong> (often cited as Nicaragua's first premium factory marca) and still a pillar of Estelí identity. Iconic lines such as Antaño, Clásico, Cuatro Cinco, Número Uno, and Cinco Décadas carry both heritage marketing and real rolling culture. Ownership and management have evolved over decades, but the factory's role as a living piece of Nicaraguan cigar history remains constant.</p>\n<p>Beyond house brands, the <a href=\"/node/joyafactory/\">Joya factory</a> is a major contract manufacturer for modern boutiques. Graph clients and partners include <a href=\"/node/dunbarton/\">Dunbarton</a> (Sobremesa and related), <a href=\"/node/viaje/\">Viaje</a>, <a href=\"/node/warped/\">Warped</a>, and others that need a historic Estelí floor with serious capacity. That dual heritage-plus-contract identity places Joya beside A.J., My Father, and Plasencia as infrastructure, not only nostalgia.</p>\n<p>On Cigar Nexus, Joya the brand and Joya the factory are linked: consumer lines on one path, boutique clients on the other. Search \"Joya de Nicaragua Antaño\" and \"where is Sobremesa made\" both belong in this cluster.</p>",
  "laranja": "<p><strong>Laranja Reserva</strong> is an <a href=\"/node/espinosa/\">Espinosa Premium Cigars</a> line named for its orange-hued Brazilian wrapper (laranja is Portuguese for orange), over Nicaraguan binder and filler. Released in 2014, it was the brand's breakthrough cigar. The family now has four blends: the original Laranja, Laranja Escuro (Brazilian Mata Fina wrapper), Laranja Azulejo (Brazilian Arapiraca binder), and Laranja Hybrid. The Hybrid debuted at PCA 2026 with an Ecuadorian Habano Oscuro wrapper and Brazilian tobacco moved into the filler.</p>\n<p>Laranja is rolled at Espinosa's own <a href=\"/node/lazona/\">La Zona</a> factory in Estelí, which <a href=\"/node/erikespinosa/\">Erik Espinosa</a> opened around 2012. When <a href=\"/node/sixzeroone/\">601</a> and <a href=\"/node/murcielago/\">Murciélago</a> moved to <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernandez</a>'s San Lotano factory in Ocotal at the end of 2016, La Zona's director of operations said the factory would remain \"home to Laranja Reserva.\" Espinosa later told Cigar Aficionado that La Zona makes <a href=\"/node/crema/\">Crema</a>, <a href=\"/node/espinosahabano/\">Espinosa Habano</a>, and Laranja.</p>\n<p>Laranja depends on one particular Brazilian wrapper. When COVID hit Brazil's growers, Espinosa made no Laranja for more than two years before resuming production with the same leaf.</p>",
  "knucklesandwich": "<p><strong>Knuckle Sandwich</strong> is the cigar brand of chef and television host <a href=\"/node/guyfieri/\">Guy Fieri</a>, developed with <a href=\"/node/erikespinosa/\">Erik Espinosa</a> over about 18 months and launched in 2022. <a href=\"/node/espinosa/\">Espinosa Premium Cigars</a> handles sales and distribution. The debut came in two blends: Habano (Ecuador Habano wrapper) and Maduro (Ecuador Sumatra wrapper), both over Nicaraguan binder and filler. A Connecticut followed, and Puro Nicaragua (2025) made it four lines.</p>\n<p>Every Knuckle Sandwich is made under contract by <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernandez</a> at his <a href=\"/node/sanlotano/\">San Lotano</a> factory in Ocotal, Nicaragua, not at Espinosa's own <a href=\"/node/lazona/\">La Zona</a>. Limited editions such as the Chef Special 2025 come from the same factory. In 2026 Fieri and Espinosa announced a new factory in Santiago, Dominican Republic with D'Crossier's Isaias Santana Diaz, and said Nicaraguan Knuckle Sandwich production would stay with A.J. Fernandez.</p>",
  "sixzeroone": "<p><strong>601</strong> is the flagship brand line of <a href=\"/node/espinosa/\">Espinosa Premium Cigars</a> and a direct descendant of the original <a href=\"/node/eobrands/\">EO Brands</a> partnership between <a href=\"/node/erikespinosa/\">Erik Espinosa</a> and Eddie Ortega. Born in the mid-2000s wave of bold Estelí profiles, 601 became a gateway full-Nicaraguan smoke: strong, well-built, and priced for everyday enthusiasts rather than allocation-only collectors. Red, Blue, Green, La Bomba, and countless limiteds expanded the family while keeping the core identity intact.</p>\n<p>Today production is centered at Espinosa's <a href=\"/node/lazona/\">La Zona</a> factory in Estelí, with historical manufacturing ties that also include <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernandez</a> facilities on the graph. That journey from EO-era contract culture to owned factory capacity is the 601 business story in miniature. Sibling Espinosa brands (<a href=\"/node/murcielago/\">Murciélago</a>, Laranja, Knuckle Sandwich, Habano, Crema) share the same ecosystem.</p>\n<p>On Cigar Nexus, 601 is the consumer-facing spearhead of the Espinosa node. Cross-link to Espinosa, La Zona, EO Brands, and Erik Espinosa for full context. SEO traffic often starts with \"601 Red\" or \"601 La Bomba\"; this page should answer ownership and factory before tasting clichés.</p>",
  "murcielago": "<p><strong>Murciélago</strong> (Spanish for \"bat\") is a line from <a href=\"/node/espinosa/\">Espinosa Premium Cigars</a> known for pairing a Mexican San Andrés wrapper with Nicaraguan binder and filler, a combination <a href=\"/node/erikespinosa/\">Erik Espinosa</a> embraced well before it became common. It debuted at the 2009 trade show as an <a href=\"/node/eobrands/\">EO Brands</a> cigar, the partnership of Espinosa and <a href=\"/node/eddieortega/\">Eddie Ortega</a>, and was originally rolled at the García family's <a href=\"/node/myfather/\">My Father Cigars</a> factory in Estelí.</p>\n<p>When EO Brands dissolved in 2012, Espinosa kept the name but not the blend, which stayed with the Garcías. Production moved to his own <a href=\"/node/lazona/\">La Zona</a> factory (with some early work at TAVICUSA), and in 2015 he relaunched a reverse-engineered Murciélago with help from Amilcar Perez-Castro, a former My Father factory manager. Since 2017 the line has been made by <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernandez</a> at the <a href=\"/node/sanlotano/\">San Lotano</a> factory in Ocotal. Erik Espinosa has called it one of his best sellers, running neck and neck with <a href=\"/node/sixzeroone/\">601</a> Blue.</p>",
  "padron1964": "<p><strong>Padrón 1964 Anniversary</strong> is the middle and most widely celebrated prestige tier of the <a href=\"/node/padron/\">Padrón</a> family, named for the year <a href=\"/node/joseopadron/\">José Orlando Padrón</a> founded the company. Introduced as a higher-aged, more refined expression than the regular numbered line, 1964 uses extensively matured Nicaraguan tobacco (often cited in the multi-year range before rolling) and is typically box-pressed in a range of vitolas with Natural and Maduro wrappers. The result is the signature Padrón balance: cocoa, earth, coffee, and cedar-like depth without rough edges, delivered with the construction consistency that made the house a critic and retailer favorite.</p>\n<p>All 1964 cigars are made at the family's <a href=\"/node/tabacoscubanica/\">Tabacos Cubanica</a> operation in Estelí under the same vertical model as the rest of the portfolio. There is no separate contract factory story and no corporate conglomerate owner. Positioning sits clearly between the approachable regular line and ultra-prestige <a href=\"/node/padron1926/\">Serie 1926</a>, which is why 1964 is often the first \"step-up\" Padrón recommended to smokers leaving milder brands.</p>\n<p>On the map, 1964 is a series node of Padrón, not an independent company. Pair it with the parent brand and 1926 when explaining the three-tier ladder, and with José Orlando's biography for why the year 1964 appears on the band. For SEO, \"Padrón 1964 vs 1926\" and \"Padrón 1964 maduro\" are high-intent queries this page should answer with factory and aging facts.</p>",
  "padron1926": "<p><strong>Padrón Serie 1926</strong> is the pinnacle of the <a href=\"/node/padron/\">Padrón</a> lineup, created to honor the <strong>1926</strong> birth year of founder <a href=\"/node/joseopadron/\">José Orlando Padrón</a>. These are among the most carefully selected and longest-aged cigars in the family's inventory: Nicaraguan tobaccos held for extended periods in Estelí aging rooms, rolled at <a href=\"/node/tabacoscubanica/\">Tabacos Cubanica</a>, then rested again as finished cigars. Natural and Maduro versions across a short list of sizes deliver the fullest expression of the house style: dense, complex, remarkably smooth for the strength, and built for slow smoking rather than novelty vitolas.</p>\n<p>Production volumes are intentionally limited relative to the regular line and even 1964, which keeps 1926 in the special-occasion and collector conversation without secondary-market gimmicks. There is no celebrity collab layer and no outside factory; the series exists to prove what multi-year family inventory can do when marketing is almost nonexistent. Critics and retailers have treated 1926 as a reference Nicaraguan puro for decades.</p>\n<p>Map Serie 1926 as the top child series of Padrón, above <a href=\"/node/padron1964/\">1964 Anniversary</a> and the numbered line. Cross-link to José Orlando (the birth year on the band) and Tabacos Cubanica (where it is made). Search comparisons \"1926 vs 1964\" should resolve here with a clear tier explanation, not vague superlatives alone.</p>",
  "davidoff": "<p><strong>Davidoff</strong> is one of the most prestigious luxury cigar brands in the world, rooted in the Geneva legacy of Zino Davidoff and today owned by Swiss group <a href=\"/node/oettinger/\">Oettinger Davidoff AG</a>. After a celebrated Cuban period, the brand's commercial path shifted following the late-1980s break with Cuban production partners; Oettinger rebuilt Davidoff as a non-Cuban luxury house with Dominican manufacturing centered at <a href=\"/node/tabadom/\">Tabadom</a> (Tabacos Dominicanos) in Villa González, the factory complex long associated with master blender Henke Kelner and the same floor that also supports <a href=\"/node/avo/\">AVO</a> and <a href=\"/node/griffins/\">The Griffin's</a>.</p>\n<p>The modern catalog spans refined Dominican classics and bolder regional projects. Flagship families include Grand Cru, Millennium, Aniversario, Winston Churchill, Late Hour, and the popular Nicaragua series that deliberately taps Central American power while keeping Davidoff's finishing and presentation standards. Limited editions, special releases, and boutique packaging reinforce the brand's luxury positioning and global duty-free / specialty retail distribution. Sister marques under Oettinger (<a href=\"/node/zino/\">Zino</a>, AVO, Griffin's, <a href=\"/node/camacho/\">Camacho</a>) cover more approachable or fuller niches so Davidoff itself can stay at the top of the price and prestige ladder.</p>\n<p>On Cigar Nexus, Davidoff is the flagship of the Swiss luxury corporate cluster: Oettinger ownership, Tabadom production for core DR lines, and selective Nicaraguan leaf programs for strength-forward SKUs. Searchers asking \"are Davidoff cigars Cuban,\" \"who owns Davidoff,\" or \"Davidoff Nicaragua vs Grand Cru\" need that ownership and factory split made explicit. Cross-link to Oettinger, Tabadom, AVO, and Zino for the full group story.</p>",
  "arturo": "<p><strong>Arturo Fuente</strong> is one of the most respected family-owned cigar houses in the world, built across generations from a 1912 founding story into a Dominican powerhouse headquartered around Santiago. Under the leadership of the Fuente family, especially Carlos Fuente Sr. and <a href=\"/node/carlitofuente/\">Carlos \"Carlito\" Fuente Jr.</a>, the company became synonymous with Cuban-seed tobacco grown outside Cuba, obsessive aging, and lines that define entire categories: Hemingway, Don Carlos, Flor Fina 8-5-8, Añejo, Gran Reserva / Reserva, and the ultra-rare <a href=\"/node/opusx/\">Fuente Fuente OpusX</a>. House production is centered at <a href=\"/node/tabafuente/\">Tabacalera A. Fuente</a>, with agricultural crown jewel Château de la Fuente supplying the estate-grown wrappers that made OpusX possible.</p>\n<p>Fuente is also a partnership engine. Longtime ally <a href=\"/node/jcnewman/\">J.C. Newman</a> of Tampa rolls Diamond Crown, MAXIMUS, Julius Caeser, Black Diamond, and related Newman marques on Fuente floors after a relationship that began in Tampa in the mid-1980s (La Unica was an early Newman brand rolled by Fuente). <a href=\"/node/ashton/\">Ashton</a>, owned by the Levin family, has relied on Fuente construction for its classic Dominican portfolio while building separate Nicaraguan sister brands elsewhere. Those contract relationships matter on the map: they show how one family factory can host outside trademarks without surrendering control of OpusX-level agricultural secrets.</p>\n<p>On Cigar Nexus, Arturo Fuente is the Dominican anchor of traditional ultra-premium craft. Follow edges to Tabacalera A. Fuente for manufacturing, to OpusX for the estate-wrapper breakthrough, and to Newman and Ashton for the two most important outside brands that share the floor. When people search \"best Dominican cigars\" or \"Fuente vs Padron,\" this node is the factual hub: family ownership, Santiago production, Château agriculture, and a partnership web that still shapes American retail.</p>",
  "opusx": "<p><strong>Fuente Fuente OpusX</strong> is the flagship ultra-premium line of <a href=\"/node/arturo/\">Arturo Fuente</a>, created by <a href=\"/node/carlitofuente/\">Carlos \"Carlito\" Fuente Jr.</a> Developed through the mid-1990s and released in November 1995 as the first commercially successful cigar to use a Cuban-seed wrapper grown in the Dominican Republic, OpusX proved that estate-level wrapper culture was possible outside Cuba. The leaf comes from the Fuentes' <strong>Château de la Fuente</strong> project, with rolling and finishing tied to <a href=\"/node/tabafuente/\">Tabacalera A. Fuente</a> in Santiago. For collectors, the double-band presentation, tiny annual volumes, and multi-year aging created a secondary market and a \"holy grail\" reputation that few brands have ever matched.</p>\n<p>OpusX is not a Nicaraguan boutique story; it is a Dominican agricultural triumph. Related Fuente programs (Perfecxion, Forbidden X, Lost City, and special anniversaries) extend the estate language without diluting the core myth. The blend is typically full, complex, and built for long aging, with power balanced by the refinement expected of Fuente construction. Trade and critic culture still treat first-growth OpusX releases as events, which keeps search demand high year after year.</p>\n<p>On the map, OpusX is a child brand of Arturo Fuente and a product of Tabacalera A. Fuente / Château agriculture. Pair this page with the parent Arturo node and the factory node when explaining why Dominican ultra-premium still competes with Estelí's boutique boom. For SEO and clarity: OpusX is Fuente family, Dominican-grown Cuban-seed wrapper, limited supply, not a corporate heritage name and not a Cuban Habanos product.</p>",
  "sancristobal": "<p><strong>San Cristóbal</strong> is an <a href=\"/node/ashton/\">Ashton</a> brand, owned by the Levin family's Ashton Distributors. Launched in 2007 as Ashton's first Nicaraguan cigar, it is made by the García family at <a href=\"/node/myfather/\">My Father Cigars</a> in Estelí. That makes it a sibling to Ashton's other García-made brand, <a href=\"/node/laaromadecuba/\">La Aroma de Cuba</a>, and part of the same partnership between the Levins and <a href=\"/node/pepin/\">Pepín García</a>.</p>\n<p>The line has grown over the years. Revelation arrived in 2013 as the brand's fourth blend, and Quintessence followed in 2016 as a medium-to-full cigar with an Ecuadorian Habano wrapper. Other San Cristóbal lines have included Elegancia, Ovation, and Of the World.</p>\n<p>Like <a href=\"/node/tatuaje/\">Tatuaje</a>, San Cristóbal is one of the outside brands that made the García factory known for contract work. An earlier version of this map also recorded a \"family connection\" between San Cristóbal and Tatuaje through Janny García. We found no source for it, so it has been removed.</p>",
  "ashton": "<p><strong>Ashton</strong> is one of the longest-running premium brands in the American market, founded by Robert Levin and still led by the Levin family. Unlike houses that chase only Nicaraguan power, Ashton built its reputation on refined, approachable Dominican profiles rolled at <a href=\"/node/tabafuente/\">Tabacalera A. Fuente</a> for the <a href=\"/node/arturo/\">Arturo Fuente</a> partnership, one of the most trusted manufacturing relationships in the industry. Flagship lines such as Classic, Cabinet Selection, Aged Maduro, VSG, ESG, and Symmetry span mild to full while keeping the brand's hallmark smoothness and consistency that made it a default recommendation in U.S. shops for decades.</p>\n<p>The Levin portfolio is dual-country by design. While core Ashton SKUs remain Dominican Fuente-made elegance, sister brands <a href=\"/node/laaromadecuba/\">La Aroma de Cuba</a> and <a href=\"/node/sancristobal/\">San Cristóbal</a> are produced at My Father in Estelí, bringing García-family Nicaraguan strength under the same ownership umbrella. That split is essential for map readers: Ashton the marca is not the same factory story as La Aroma de Cuba, even when both appear in one corporate catalog.</p>\n<p>On Cigar Nexus, Ashton sits at the intersection of Levin ownership, Fuente Dominican craft, and García Nicaraguan sister brands. Search traffic often starts with \"Ashton VSG\" or \"Ashton vs Padron\"; this page answers with factory truth, product-line map, and the dual-country strategy that keeps the brand relevant from Connecticut-forward classics to full-bodied specials.</p>",
  "rockypatel": "<p><strong>Rocky Patel Premium Cigars</strong> is the company founded by <a href=\"/node/rakeshpatel/\">Rocky Patel</a>, an Indian-American former Los Angeles lawyer who started Indian Tabac in 1995 and put his own name on the catalog in 2003. Decade, Vintage, The Edge, Sun Grown, Fifteenth Anniversary, and a large limited program made it a default U.S. humidor brand. The model is marketing energy plus multi-factory production, not a single tiny room.</p>\n<p>Honduras volume still runs through a long partnership with <a href=\"/node/plasencia/\">Plasencia</a> at <a href=\"/node/plasenciahonduras/\">El Paraíso</a>. Nicaragua sits at owned <a href=\"/node/tavicusa/\">TaviCusa</a> in Estelí, with family oversight including brother <a href=\"/node/nishpatel/\">Nish Patel</a>. That dual structure (partner grower-manufacturer plus owned Estelí floor) is how one brand fields so many SKUs. The company is independent of STG and Altadis. Who makes Rocky Patel cigars? Plasencia in Honduras and TaviCusa in Nicaragua, under Rocky's company.</p>",
  "illusione": "<p><strong>Illusione Cigars</strong>, founded by <a href=\"/node/diongiolito/\">Dion Giolito</a>, is the cult \"quiet boutique\": understated bands, meticulous blending, and Nicaraguan cigars built for aging rather than Instagram shock. Core documents such as the Original Document series, Singularé, 88, cg4, Epernay, and related releases earned a reputation for structure, medium-plus to full body, and leaf selection obsessive enough that fans treat Illusione as a reference Aganorsa-era brand.</p>\n<p>Long-term production and tobacco relationships center on <a href=\"/node/tabsa/\">TABSA</a> / <a href=\"/node/aganorsa/\">Aganorsa Leaf</a> in Estelí, with some work also at <a href=\"/node/sanlotano/\">San Lotano</a> (A.J. Fernandez) and <a href=\"/node/joyafactory/\">Joya</a>. That places Illusione beside Viaje and Warped in the TABSA gravity well, distinct from My Father-centric boutiques like Crowned Heads or Tatuaje. No celebrity collabs, no infused lane: the brand's identity is tobacco seriousness.</p>\n<p>Map Illusione as Dion Giolito's marca with TABSA as the primary factory edge. Cluster it with Viaje and Warped for Aganorsa boutique culture, and against Foundation/Crowned Heads when explaining different Estelí contract \"homes\" for U.S. indie brands.</p>",
  "viaje": "<p><strong>Viaje Cigars</strong>, founded by <a href=\"/node/andrefarkas/\">Andre Farkas</a>, is the scarcity-first boutique that turned limited releases into events. Creative vitolas, seasonal drops (Summerfest and kin), and lines such as Exclusivo, Double Ligero, Placeres, and Oro often sell through quickly, training retailers and collectors to treat Viaje as allocation culture rather than everyday catalog filler. The brand helped define how 2000s–2010s boutiques could market rarity without being a legacy Cuban name.</p>\n<p>Production jumps across top Estelí floors by design. Frequent partners include <a href=\"/node/tabsa/\">TABSA</a> (Aganorsa), <a href=\"/node/joyafactory/\">Joya de Nicaragua</a>, and <a href=\"/node/lazona/\">La Zona</a> (Espinosa). That multi-factory graph is the point: Farkas matches blend experiments to the right room instead of forcing one house style. Peers on the map include Illusione and HVC in the same entrepreneurial Estelí layer.</p>\n<p>On Cigar Nexus, Viaje is the limited-release archetype of the boutique cluster. Cross-link to Andre Farkas and the factory edges above; SEO queries about \"Viaje Summerfest,\" \"Viaje Exclusivo,\" and \"who makes Viaje\" need both the scarcity story and the real production web.</p>\n<p>Viaje has also used Dominican rooms, with TNT and C4 made at <a href=\"/node/pdr/\">PDR</a> in the mid-2010s and New Beginnings and Mofongo at <a href=\"/node/laisla/\">Tabacalera La iSLA</a>.</p>",
  "camacho": "<p><strong>Camacho</strong> is the Honduran brand owned by <a href=\"/node/oettinger/\">Oettinger Davidoff</a>. Cuban exile Simon Camacho opened a factory in Miami in <strong>1961</strong>. After he died, Caribe Imported Cigars and the <a href=\"/node/eiroafamily/\">Eiroa family</a> took the company. Oettinger dates that purchase to <strong>1994</strong>. <a href=\"/node/christianeiroa/\">Christian Eiroa</a> has said 1995. The Camacho that smokers know was built after that, in Honduras. Julio Eiroa grew Authentic Corojo in the Jamastran Valley, from the seed line Daniel Rodríguez brought to Honduras from Cuba's El Corojo farm. Christian ran Caribe in Miami and, around 2000, put Camacho Corojo on the shelf. Rolling was at Tabacos Rancho Jamastran in Danlí, where the family also made Baccarat and La Fontana. Diploma was the upper-priming line from those Corojo years.</p>\n<p>The sale that split the story closed on <strong>1 October 2008</strong> and was announced on 13 October. Oettinger bought the company and the Rancho Jamastran factory. The farms stayed with the Eiroas. Christian, then 36, stayed president and reported to chief executive Reto Cina. Julio left the company and kept growing in the valley. A ten-year tobacco purchase agreement covered Eiroa leaf and was not renewed. Christian had founded <a href=\"/node/cle/\">CLE Cigar Company</a> by May 2012. CLE's first cigars were rolled at Rancho Jamastran, by then a Davidoff factory. CLE and Aladino now roll at the family's own <a href=\"/node/eiroadanli/\">Danlí factory</a>. That floor is not where Camacho is made.</p>\n<p>In 2013 the brand reset the catalog and the packaging, and the scorpion became the mark. Davidoff then built Diadema Cigars de Honduras on the outskirts of Danlí. The plant was announced in <strong>2015</strong>, rolling was underway in 2016, and it was the working Camacho factory by 2017. In 2015 the group also bought Finca La Merced, an 81-acre farm about 45 minutes from Danlí, to grow its own criollo and corojo. That farm does not make every Camacho an estate puro. Camacho is not rolled at <a href=\"/node/tabadom/\">Tabadom</a>. Corojo is still the power line. Connecticut is the milder line. Triple Maduro is the darker, sweeter line. Ecuador and Broadleaf sit beside them on the shelf. <strong>Liberty</strong> began in 2002 as a Fourth of July release, first in five sizes. Most years since have been one vitola, often the 11/18 figurado named for Christian Eiroa's mother's birthday on November 18. American Barrel Aged, Coyolar, Criollo, and Nicaragua Barrel Aged left the U.S. price list in October 2022.</p>\n<p>Who owns Camacho? Oettinger Davidoff, the same Swiss parent as <a href=\"/node/davidoff/\">Davidoff</a>, <a href=\"/node/avo/\">AVO</a>, <a href=\"/node/griffins/\">The Griffin's</a>, and <a href=\"/node/zino/\">Zino</a>. Davidoff, AVO, and Griffin's are the Dominican sisters at Tabadom. Zino is the other Central American brand in the group. Camacho is the Honduran one, on Davidoff's own Danlí floor. It is not an STG or Forged marca, and it is not a CLE cigar.</p>",
  "laaromadecuba": "<p><strong>La Aroma de Cuba</strong> is the Levin family / <a href=\"/node/ashton/\">Ashton</a> Nicaraguan sister brand, produced by the García family at <a href=\"/node/myfather/\">My Father</a> in Estelí. Where core Ashton lines are famous for refined Dominican elegance at <a href=\"/node/tabafuente/\">Tabacalera A. Fuente</a>, La Aroma de Cuba is the fuller, Nicaraguan-power half of the dual-country strategy: same ownership umbrella, different factory culture and leaf profile.</p>\n<p>Popular lines such as Edición Especial, Mi Amor, Noblesse, Pasión, and Connecticut-forward expressions made the brand a high-value recommendation for smokers stepping up from mild Dominicans without jumping into boutique allocation chaos. Construction quality tracks the My Father / Pepín García reputation that also supports <a href=\"/node/tatuaje/\">Tatuaje</a> and <a href=\"/node/crownedheads/\">Crowned Heads</a> on this map.</p>\n<p>On Cigar Nexus, La Aroma de Cuba is ownership (Ashton/Levin) + manufacture (My Father). Do not confuse it with Cuban Habanos product of the same historic name. SEO: \"La Aroma de Cuba,\" \"La Aroma de Cuba Mi Amor,\" \"Ashton La Aroma de Cuba factory.\"</p>",
  "alecbradley": "<p><strong>Alec Bradley</strong> is the company Alan Rubin founded in 1996 after selling his family's hardware and fasteners business, naming it for his sons Alec and Bradley. Its broad catalog includes Prensado, Black Market, Tempus, Magic Toast, Kintsugi, and American Classic. The Prensado Churchill was Cigar Aficionado's No. 1 Cigar of the Year for 2011, rated 96 points.</p>\n<p>Rubin's company worked as a blending and marketing house that used partner factories rather than owning one. Many Alec Bradley lines are rolled at <a href=\"/node/raicescubanas/\">Raíces Cubanas</a> in Danlí, Honduras, the Endemaño family factory, and in 2013 Alec Bradley even distributed a factory-named cigar, Raices Cubanas 1941. Ralph Montero, Alec Bradley's executive vice-president and partner, spent two decades with the company and was credited with running its products.</p>\n<p>In 2023 <a href=\"/node/stg/\">Scandinavian Tobacco Group</a>, owner of <a href=\"/node/generalcigar/\">General Cigar</a>, bought Alec Bradley. Montero worked briefly with STG, then in November 2024 rejoined the Endemaños as Raíces Cubanas launched its own four-line portfolio. That move links the brand's past directly to its main factory.</p>",
  "lapalina": "<p>La Palina is one of the most historically significant American cigar marcas, tracing to <strong>1896</strong> when Ukrainian émigré Samuel Paley founded the <strong>Congress Cigar Factory</strong> in Chicago, the city that was then home to hundreds of cigar operations. Paley named his first cigar range <strong>La Palina</strong> in honor of his wife, <strong>Goldie Drell Paley</strong>; at its height Congress rolled roughly <strong>one million cigars per day</strong>. The Great Depression eventually closed the factory, but not before Paley family capital helped launch what became <strong>CBS</strong> under Sam's son William S. Paley, linking America's broadcast era to one of its great Gilded Age cigarmakers.</p>\n<p>William \"Bill\" Paley, Sam's great-grandson, revived the brand in <strong>2010</strong> on a New York rooftop as a personal luxury cigar project, after an early, short-lived collaboration with Graycliff in the Bahamas tied to the family's Lyford Cay estate. La Palina 2.0 is a <strong>negociant</strong>: it does not own a factory, but it does own its tobacco, finished products, and brands. Five years after relaunch, Bill partnered with veterans <strong>Samuel \"Sammy\" Phillips</strong> (Rocky Patel, Alec Bradley) and <strong>Clay Roberts</strong> (Alec Bradley, A.J. Fernandez); today they steward operations while Bill remains chairman. Portfolio cigars are crafted in <strong>Honduras</strong>, <strong>Nicaragua</strong> with <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernandez</a>, the <strong>Dominican Republic</strong> (including <a href=\"/node/pdr/\">PDR Cigars</a>), and <strong>Miami</strong>: where the limited-production <strong>Goldie</strong> line is rolled at <a href=\"/node/eltitandebronze/\">El Titan de Bronze</a>. Current releases span Goldie, Mr. Sam, Classic, White/Black/Blue/Bronze/Silver Label, Family Series Miami, La Palina 1948, and the <strong>125 Anos</strong> anniversary edition.</p>",
  "westtampa": "<p><strong>West Tampa Tobacco Co.</strong> is the boutique brand co-founded by master blender <a href=\"/node/rickrodriguez/\">Rick Rodriguez</a> (with family, including daughter Sara in the public story), launched after Rick's nearly three-decade run as CAO's blender and brand ambassador under <a href=\"/node/generalcigar/\">General Cigar</a> / STG. Named for Tampa's historic cigar district and Rick's family rolling heritage, West Tampa focuses on bold, modern Nicaraguan-forward blends (White, Red, and Attic Series / limiteds among the lines collectors chase) rather than mild Connecticut catalog filler.</p>\n<p>Production is handmade in Nicaragua at boutique-oriented capacity; graph and trade coverage commonly place core work at <a href=\"/node/garmendia/\">Garmendia</a> and related Estelí partners, distinct from CAO's corporate STG floors. The brand is independent of General Cigar: Rick left the corporate system to build something family-run, which is why West Tampa appears as a separate map node from CAO even though the blender history connects them.</p>\n<p>SEO: \"West Tampa Tobacco,\" \"West Tampa Red,\" \"Rick Rodriguez West Tampa,\" \"West Tampa factory.\" Cross-link Rick Rodriguez, CAO (history), and Garmendia when present.</p>",
  "avo": "<p><strong>AVO</strong> is the premium Dominican brand born from the friendship of Armenian-American jazz pianist and composer <strong>Avo Uvezian</strong> and master blender <strong>Hendrik \"Henke\" Kelner</strong>. Uvezian, already a celebrated musician who had spent years in Puerto Rico and the Caribbean, set out in the 1980s to make a cigar that matched his ear for composition. He was introduced to Kelner, then building what would become <a href=\"/node/tabadom/\">Tabacos Dominicanos (Tabadom)</a> in Villa González. Their first commercial year produced a small run (Kelner later recalled roughly 17,000 cigars that Uvezian carried out under his arms). The Davidoff store on Madison Avenue began carrying the brand in <strong>1988</strong>, and by the mid-1990s U.S. demand had soared. After the launch of AVO Maduro, Oettinger approached Uvezian about purchasing the marca; the deal kept his name on the band and left him active with the brand. AVO has been part of the <a href=\"/node/oettinger/\">Oettinger Davidoff</a> portfolio ever since, while Uvezian's musical persona (he died in 2017 at 91) still shapes packaging, limited editions, and the brand's annual LE tradition.</p>\n<p>Production remains at Tabadom under Swiss group quality systems, the same Dominican floor that also rolls much of the <a href=\"/node/davidoff/\">Davidoff</a> and <a href=\"/node/griffins/\">Griffin's</a> portfolios. Core lines emphasize balance over raw power. <strong>Classic</strong> is the mild-to-medium gateway (often an Ecuador Connecticut wrapper over Dominican long fillers), with a parallel <strong>Classic Maduro</strong> for darker sweetness. <strong>XO</strong> steps up with longer-aged Dominican filler and binder under a high-grade Ecuador Connecticut leaf, frequently cited for six-year aged components and a creamy, woody profile. <strong>Heritage</strong> brings Ecuador sun-grown wrapper character and a warmer medium body. The <strong>Syncro</strong> family (including Syncro Nicaragua and Fogata) deliberately folds Nicaraguan leaf into the Dominican recipe for a more modern, fuller edge without abandoning AVO's polished identity. Seasonal and anniversary projects (Improvisation, Domaine, Signature and Maduro anniversary releases, and the annual limited editions Uvezian pioneered) keep the catalog in motion for collectors.</p>\n<p>On the map, AVO is how a musician's personal project became a permanent Oettinger luxury node: not a boutique Estelí startup, but a Dominican-made, Swiss-owned brand that still sells the idea of jazz composition in every band name. It sits between approachable Griffin's and flagship Davidoff in the same corporate/manufacturing cluster, proof that Tabadom can host both gateway and luxury SKUs under one roof. For enthusiasts following ownership trees, AVO is owned by Oettinger, made at Tabadom, and historically inseparable from Kelner's Dominican factory story, even after the factory itself passed fully into Davidoff-group hands.</p>",
  "griffins": "<p><strong>The Griffin's</strong> is a historic Dominican brand under <a href=\"/node/oettinger/\">Oettinger Davidoff</a>, known for elegant, mild-to-medium profiles aimed at smokers who want refinement without heavy strength. The marca is closely tied to Henke Kelner's early Dominican work: Kelner developed and expanded The Griffin's at his Tabacos Dominicanos operation in the mid-1980s, before the full Davidoff-era corporate consolidation that made <a href=\"/node/tabadom/\">Tabadom</a> the group's Dominican manufacturing backbone. Production today still sits on that Villa González infrastructure shared with <a href=\"/node/davidoff/\">Davidoff</a> and <a href=\"/node/avo/\">AVO</a>.</p>\n<p>Core lines such as Classic, Vintage, Escudo, and Naturale emphasize balance, creaminess, and consistent construction rather than boutique shock value. The Griffin's helped establish the Dominican Republic's reputation for approachable premium cigars long before Nicaragua's modern boom dominated enthusiast conversation. In the Oettinger portfolio it sits below ultra-luxury Davidoff and beside AVO's jazz-branded elegance as a quieter, everyday-to-special-occasion Dominican option.</p>\n<p>Map The Griffin's as an Oettinger marca made at Tabadom, not a Cuban heritage trademark and not an independent boutique. Pair it with Davidoff and AVO when explaining how one Dominican factory hosts multiple Swiss-group brands at different price and intensity tiers. For SEO, mild Dominican + Kelner/Tabadom history + Oettinger ownership are the high-intent facts retailer pages often skip.</p>",
  "zino": "<p><strong>Zino</strong> is the Oettinger Davidoff group brand named for Zino Davidoff himself, positioned as a more approachable premium alternative to flagship <a href=\"/node/davidoff/\">Davidoff</a> pricing while still carrying Swiss-group quality expectations. Where Davidoff often leads with Dominican ultra-luxury presentation, Zino's best-known modern identity includes Honduran and Nicaraguan programs: Zino Platinum (Scepter and related lines that helped define early-2000s thick-ring value luxury), Zino Nicaragua, and Zino Honduras expressions that span medium to fuller profiles without abandoning everyday smokeability.</p>\n<p>Corporate ownership runs through <a href=\"/node/oettinger/\">Oettinger Davidoff</a>. That multi-country story is intentional: Zino covers Central American flavor and value while Davidoff and AVO hold much of the Dominican luxury lane, and <a href=\"/node/camacho/\">Camacho</a> occupies the bolder Honduran Corojo end of the same corporate family.</p>\n<p>On Cigar Nexus, Zino is the accessible rung of the Oettinger ladder. Cross-link to Davidoff for the namesake and prestige parent brand, to Oettinger for ownership, and to Camacho when comparing Honduran power brands inside the same Swiss portfolio. Search queries like \"Zino Platinum,\" \"Zino vs Davidoff,\" and \"who makes Zino cigars\" resolve here with ownership and factory clarity.</p>",
  "macanudo": "<p><strong>Macanudo</strong> is one of the best-selling premium cigar brands in the United States, produced and distributed by <a href=\"/node/generalcigar/\">General Cigar</a> under <a href=\"/node/stg/\">Scandinavian Tobacco Group</a>. The modern brand was launched for the U.S. market in <strong>1971</strong> after General acquired Temple Hall in Kingston, Jamaica (1969) and reworked the marca with legends including Alfons Mayer and Ramón Cifuentes. Early Café blends paired Connecticut Shade wrappers (from General/Cullman shade culture and proprietary seed lines such as 211 and 8212) with Dominican, Jamaican, and Mexican tobaccos, creating the mild-to-medium \"everyday premium\" profile that defined American humidor shelves for decades.</p>\n<p>Production later consolidated in the Dominican Republic after Jamaica operations closed around <strong>2000</strong>, while the catalog expanded far beyond Café: Maduro, Gold Label / Vintage programs, Cru Royale, Inspirado, Estate Reserve, and numerous limiteds keep the brand relevant for smokers who want consistency more than boutique scarcity. Map edges also show STG Nicaraguan capacity (including <a href=\"/node/stgesteli/\">STG Estelí</a> and related network routing) for select SKUs, so Macanudo is no longer only a single-country mild Connecticut story. Scale, national distribution, and brand recognition remain the point: Macanudo is STG's accessible face beside bolder siblings like <a href=\"/node/cao/\">CAO</a> and <a href=\"/node/lagloriacubana/\">La Gloria Cubana</a>.</p>\n<p>For searchers asking \"is Macanudo mild,\" \"who makes Macanudo,\" or \"Macanudo vs Montecristo,\" the answers are ownership (General/STG, not Altadis), factory geography (historic Jamaica to Dominican volume plus STG network), and product ladder (Café gateway through fuller Inspirado/limited lines). On Cigar Nexus, Macanudo is the volume anchor of the STG classics cluster and a hub for internal links to General Cigar, STG, and sister heritage brands.</p>",
  "cao": "<p><strong>CAO</strong> is the bold, design-forward brand that helped pull a younger generation into full-flavored premium cigars in the 1990s and early 2000s. Founded as an independent creative house and later absorbed into <a href=\"/node/generalcigar/\">General Cigar</a> / <a href=\"/node/stg/\">Scandinavian Tobacco Group</a>, CAO became famous for loud packaging, country-themed lines, and blends that refused mild Dominican comfort. Core and cult expressions include Brazilia, Ecuador, Italia, Flathead, MX2, Consigliere, and Conquest-era projects, many leaning Nicaraguan and multi-origin strength rather than Connecticut mildness.</p>\n<p>The human face of CAO for nearly three decades was master blender and ambassador <a href=\"/node/rickrodriguez/\">Rick Rodriguez</a>, who later founded <a href=\"/node/westtampa/\">West Tampa Tobacco Co.</a> after leaving the corporate system. His tenure links CAO's cult years to General's distribution muscle. Production and related facilities on the map include Nicaraguan capacity and the <a href=\"/node/caoamerican/\">CAO American Caribbean</a> node for select routing, reflecting STG's multi-factory model rather than a single boutique floor. Historical blender credits on the graph also include talent such as Esteban Disla for certain projects, showing how corporate brands still pull from Estelí's freelance and factory talent pool.</p>\n<p>On Cigar Nexus, CAO is STG's creative counterweight to Macanudo's mild classic image: same parent, different smoker. Pair this page with Rick Rodriguez, West Tampa, General Cigar, and STG when tracing how an irreverent 1990s brand became a permanent corporate SKU engine without losing its loud shelf identity.</p>\n<p>CAO cigars are rolled in Estelí at Scandinavian Tobacco Group's <a href=\"/node/stgesteli/\">STG Estelí</a>, and some lines, such as CAO Arcana Firewalker, come from <a href=\"/node/caoamerican/\">American Caribbean Cigars</a>.</p>",
  "cohiba_nc": "<p><strong>Cohiba (Non-Cuban)</strong> is the U.S.-market Cohiba controlled by <a href=\"/node/stg/\">Scandinavian Tobacco Group</a> and sold today primarily through <a href=\"/node/forged/\">Forged Cigar Co.</a> (with sister routing historically through <a href=\"/node/generalcigar/\">General Cigar</a>). It is <em>not</em> the Cuban Cohiba of Habanos S.A. Decades of trademark litigation left two legal systems: Cuban Cohiba for much of the world, and General/STG Cohiba for the United States and other markets where the General trademark holds. That split is the single most important fact for any shopper who sees the yellow-and-black band on a U.S. shelf.</p>\n<p>Non-Cuban Cohiba has long mixed Dominican and Nicaraguan capacity. On this map, production and portfolio routing touch <a href=\"/node/stgesteli/\">STG Estelí</a>. Core expressions range from milder classic and Red Dot-style profiles aimed at everyday retail to fuller Blue Dot, Black, and Nicaragua-forward lines built for smokers who want power under a name they already know. Packaging, pricing, and national distribution reflect STG scale rather than boutique scarcity.</p>\n<p>For Cigar Nexus, Cohiba (NC) anchors the STG side of the Cuban-legacy trademark split. It sits with <a href=\"/node/partagas_nc/\">Partagas (NC)</a> and <a href=\"/node/punch_nc/\">Punch (NC)</a> under the Danish corporate umbrella, while <a href=\"/node/altadis/\">Altadis U.S.A.</a> (Imperial Brands) holds the parallel U.S. franchises for Montecristo and Romeo y Julieta.</p>\n<p>Shoppers comparing a yellow-banded U.S. Cohiba to a Cuban box should treat them as different products with different leaf, factories, and legal owners. Non-Cuban Cohiba cannot substitute for Habanos production or aging culture; it is a parallel marca built for markets where General/STG holds the trademark. Read the band for Forged or General routing, then the box for Dominican or Nicaraguan origin, and you already know more than most retail shelves imply.</p>",
  "partagas_nc": "<p><strong>Partagas (Non-Cuban)</strong> is a <a href=\"/node/generalcigar/\">General Cigar</a> / <a href=\"/node/stg/\">STG</a> heritage brand, completely separate from Habanos S.A.'s Cuban Partagás. After the Revolution, Ramón Cifuentes left Cuba rather than run the nationalized industry. In <strong>1974</strong> the Cifuentes family licensed Partagas manufacture to General Cigar; the first non-Cuban Partagas reached market in <strong>1977</strong> from Jamaica before production shifted to the Dominican Republic, long associated with Cameroon wrappers, Mexican binders, and Dominican (and later multi-origin) fillers. That Dominican identity made Partagas one of the best-selling premium names in the U.S. long before the boutique boom.</p>\n<p>Today the portfolio spans classic black-band expressions, fuller specials, and newer experiments such as Connecticut-wrapped and Nicaraguan-forward SKUs (including Cortado and other limited lines covered in the trade press). Manufacturing still lives inside the STG network rather than a single boutique floor. Recent STG U.S. portfolio shuffles have moved Partagas onto the General Cigar sales team (while <a href=\"/node/cohiba_nc/\">Cohiba</a> and <a href=\"/node/punch_nc/\">Punch</a> shifted toward Forged), so retail routing can change even when corporate ownership stays Danish.</p>\n<p>On the map, Partagas (NC) is a textbook embargo-era marca: Cuban name, American trademark path, Caribbean and Central American tobacco, STG distribution muscle. Pair it with Cohiba (NC) and Punch (NC) for the STG heritage cluster, and with <a href=\"/node/montecristonc/\">Montecristo (NC)</a> / <a href=\"/node/ryjnc/\">Romeo y Julieta (NC)</a> when explaining why two different conglomerates own different famous Cuban names in the U.S.</p><p>Partagas remains a daily medium-to-full benchmark in many U.S. humidors because the name is historic and the non-Cuban execution stayed widely available for decades. Cameroon-wrapper lore from the Dominican era still defines the brand's classic reputation, while newer SKUs chase Connecticut mildness or Nicaraguan strength without abandoning the black-and-gold identity. For map readers, Partagas is how STG keeps a Cuban-era name commercially alive with Caribbean and Central American tobacco rather than Havana leaf.</p>",
  "punch_nc": "<p><strong>Punch (Non-Cuban)</strong> carries a 19th-century Cuban-associated name into the U.S. market under <a href=\"/node/stg/\">Scandinavian Tobacco Group</a>, sold in recent portfolio alignment primarily through <a href=\"/node/forged/\">Forged Cigar Co.</a> It is legally and operationally separate from Habanos S.A.'s Cuban Punch. After Castro nationalized the industry, Punch owner Fernando Palacios left Cuba; the U.S. trademark path ran through Villazon &amp; Co., which built a Honduras-centered parallel brand. <a href=\"/node/generalcigar/\">General Cigar</a> acquired Villazon and its brands in <strong>1997</strong>, folding Punch into the large American heritage portfolio that later entered STG.</p>\n<p>Non-Cuban Punch is known for traditional, full-flavored, value-forward smoking: spicy, earthy profiles that thrived in the 1990s cigar boom and remain everyday retail staples. Core lines include classic Punch, Punch Deluxe, Punch Signature, Rare Corojo, and Gran Puro / Gran Puro Nicaragua extensions. Much volume has long been associated with Honduras (including STG Danlí / HATSA capacity for Nicaraguan-leaf projects) alongside Nicaraguan routing through facilities such as <a href=\"/node/stgesteli/\">STG Estelí</a> on this map. That Danlí floor also rolls non-Cuban <a href=\"/node/bolivar_nc/\">Bolivar</a>. The brand's job is consistency and scale, not boutique scarcity.</p>\n<p>Map readers should treat Punch (NC) as corporate heritage on the STG side of the Cuban-name split: same umbrella as <a href=\"/node/cohiba_nc/\">Cohiba (NC)</a>, <a href=\"/node/partagas_nc/\">Partagas (NC)</a>, <a href=\"/node/macanudo/\">Macanudo</a>, and <a href=\"/node/cao/\">CAO</a>, opposite <a href=\"/node/altadis/\">Altadis</a>-held Montecristo and Romeo y Julieta. Factory edges on the graph explain where the cigars are rolled; the Forged/General sales labels explain which STG commercial team carries the SKU this year.</p><p>Punch is less glamorous in media coverage than Cohiba but often more present in everyday U.S. retail. Gran Puro, Rare Corojo, Signature, and Deluxe give the coat of arms real blend diversity at accessible prices. Compare Punch to boutique Estelí names and you are usually comparing corporate scale and Honduran/Nicaraguan value engineering to small-batch storytelling. The graph shows Villazon history, STG ownership, and the factory edges that keep the brand on pegs nationwide.</p>",
  "trinidadespiritu": "<p>Trinidad Espiritu is <a href=\"/node/altadis/\">Altadis U.S.A.</a>'s bold reimagining of the Trinidad marque for the American market, a collaboration between Tabacalera USA product chief Rafael Nodal and master blender <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernandez</a>, rolled at the <a href=\"/node/sanlotano/\">San Lotano</a> factory in Estelí. Launched in <strong>2019</strong> as Series No. 1 (all-Nicaraguan), the line expanded into a numbered trilogy: No. 2 highlights Brazilian Arapiraca wrapper leaf, and No. 3 explores Mexican tobacco, each in festive Caribbean-themed packaging distinct from the separate Dominican <em>Trinidad Santiago</em> line.</p>\n<p>Series No. 1 Belicoso landed on <em>Cigar Aficionado</em>'s Top 25 list for 2023; Series No. 3 Belicoso followed in 2025. The limited <strong>Miami Edition</strong> (2025) is a Nicaraguan puro made at San Lotano despite Miami-inspired artwork, capturing the city's energy rather than Little Havana production. Nodal and Fernandez previously partnered on Montecristo Nicaragua and Romeo San Andrés, making Trinidad Espiritu part of a broader Altadis strategy to revive heritage names through Nicaraguan fire and modern blending partnerships.</p>",
  "cain": "<p><strong>Cain</strong> is <a href=\"/node/oliva/\">Oliva Cigar Co.</a>'s ligero-forward sub-brand, launched at the <strong>2009</strong> IPCPR show and rolled at <a href=\"/node/tabolisa/\">Tabolisa</a> in Estelí. Conceived in the same Studio Tobac wave as <a href=\"/node/nub/\">NUB</a> (Sam Leccia's Oliva period), Cain targets smokers who want unapologetic Nicaraguan strength: long-filler heavy on triple-fermented ligero from Estelí, Condega, and Jalapa, engineered for power with as much smoothness as high-ligero blends allow.</p>\n<p>Flagship <strong>Serie F</strong> (\"Forced Refined\") pushed all-ligero filler recipes under Nicaraguan Habano wrappers, with vitolas often named for ring gauge (550, 660, and kin). The line expanded into Black (Cuban-seed Nicaraguan wrapper stories), Habano, Maduro, milder Connecticut CT, Daytona, and the broader 550 family. <strong>Cain NUB</strong> crossovers marry Cain blends to NUB's short fat formats. Together NUB and Cain show Oliva segmenting innovation: shape disruption on one side, strength disruption on the other, same industrial-quality floor.</p>\n<p>On the map, Cain is an Oliva brand at Tabolisa, linked to NUB as Studio Tobac sibling. Search queries \"Cain Serie F,\" \"strongest Oliva cigar,\" and \"Cain vs Serie V\" should land here with clear portfolio position: Cain is the ligero specialist, Serie V/Melanio the flagship house lines, NUB the format experiment.</p>",
  "nub": "<p><strong>NUB</strong> is <a href=\"/node/oliva/\">Oliva Cigar Co.</a>'s short-format sub-brand, created by blender Sam Leccia and launched in <strong>2008</strong> under Oliva's Studio Tobac innovation arm. The idea was blunt and sticky: many smokers love the final third of a cigar most, so pack roughly full-size tobacco mass into a stubby ~4-inch, large-ring vitola and start at the sweet spot. Shapes branded as 460, 464, 358, and cousins became an industry talking point overnight and earned early critical love (including a <em>Cigar Insider</em> Best Buy in the launch era).</p>\n<p>Construction and blends are rolled at <a href=\"/node/tabolisa/\">Tabolisa</a> in Estelí. Core recipes keep Nicaraguan filler and binder under wrapper variants: Connecticut, Cameroon, Habano, Maduro (often Brazilian Arapiraca), and Dub double maduro. <strong>Nuance</strong> extends the marca into coffee-dessert territory (Single, Double, Triple Roast). Crossover <strong>Cain NUB</strong> sticks borrow ligero power from sibling brand <a href=\"/node/cain/\">Cain</a> for smokers who want the fat format with more force. NUB proved Oliva could invent category-shifting SKUs without leaving its own factory.</p>\n<p>Map NUB as an Oliva portfolio brand manufactured at Tabolisa, sibling to Cain, not an independent boutique. For SEO, \"NUB cigar size,\" \"NUB vs traditional robusto,\" and \"who makes NUB\" resolve to Oliva ownership plus the short-format concept story.</p>",
  "lagloriacubana": "<p><strong>La Gloria Cubana (non-Cuban)</strong> is the full-flavored General Cigar / <a href=\"/node/stg/\">STG</a> brand that began as a Miami Little Havana story and became a national powerhouse. The Perez-Carrillo family launched the modern U.S. marca from El Credito in the 1970s; <a href=\"/node/ernestoperezcarrillo/\">Ernesto Perez-Carrillo</a> took the blending reins and, by the early 1990s, La Gloria Cubana was a critical and cult sensation (including major <em>Cigar Aficionado</em> attention). Demand forced a move from Miami-scale production toward Dominican capacity. In <strong>1999</strong>, the brand (and El Credito story) entered the General Cigar / Swedish Match universe; Ernesto stayed about a decade as blender and ambassador before leaving in <strong>2009</strong> to found what is now <a href=\"/node/casacarrillo/\">Casa Carrillo</a>.</p>\n<p>Under corporate ownership the brand kept its reputation for strength and value. <strong>Serie R</strong> helped normalize large ring gauges (including regular-production 6x60 territory) in the early 2000s, while Classic / Wavell-era blends, Maduro variants, and later lines such as Intención, Los Gloriosos, and anniversary projects keep the catalog moving. Factory routing on this map includes Dominican and STG Nicaraguan capacity (<a href=\"/node/stgesteli/\">STG Estelí</a> among other edges). A 2017 Colección Reserva collaboration brought Ernesto back into a limited partnership with General, underlining that the brand's DNA still points to the Perez-Carrillo years even when legal ownership is STG.</p>\n<p>La Gloria Cubana sits in the fuller-bodied tier of the STG classics cluster opposite mild <a href=\"/node/macanudo/\">Macanudo</a> and creative <a href=\"/node/cao/\">CAO</a>. Map it with Ernesto Perez-Carrillo and Casa Carrillo for the human dynasty, and with General/STG for who owns the trademark today. For SEO, disambiguate from any Cuban La Gloria Cubana Habanos product: this page is the U.S. non-Cuban marca born in Miami and scaled by General.</p>",
  "room101": "<p><strong>Room101</strong> is the irreverent, design-obsessed brand founded by Matt Booth that built a cult following on dark humor, tattoo-adjacent aesthetics, and full-flavored blends that never pretended to be stuffy heritage. After years as an independent / boutique-leaning label (with complex distribution history), <a href=\"/node/stg/\">Scandinavian Tobacco Group</a> acquired Room101 in <strong>2022</strong>. On this map it is sold through <a href=\"/node/generalcigar/\">General Cigar</a> in the post-acquisition portfolio alignment, placing a once-outsider marca inside the same commercial system as Macanudo and Partagas while trying to keep the edgy brand voice intact.</p>\n<p>Production for Room101 projects has included Honduran and Nicaraguan capacity in the STG network, with edges on this graph to <a href=\"/node/stgdanli/\">STG Danlí (HATSA)</a>, related Honduran partners such as the Eiroa Danlí cluster for certain periods, and <a href=\"/node/stgesteli/\">STG Estelí</a>. That multi-country routing is typical of STG boutique acquisitions: keep the brand personality, plug into corporate leaf and factory scale. Lines and limiteds rotate quickly (Farce, Hit &amp; Run, Name Dropper, and many short-run designs), so the SEO value of this page is ownership + factory truth more than a frozen SKU list.</p>\n<p>Room101 shows how STG buys culture as well as volume. Cross-link to STG and General for the corporate tree, and to STG factories for where the cigars actually roll. For enthusiasts who followed Booth's independent years, the acquisition is the map event; for new shoppers, Room101 is simply the loudest face on an otherwise traditional General shelf.</p>",
  "jcnewman": "<p><strong>J.C. Newman Cigar Co.</strong> is America's oldest family-owned premium cigar company, founded in <strong>1895</strong> in Cleveland by Julius Caeser Newman and now headquartered in Tampa, Florida, in the historic El Reloj factory complex. The Newman family survived Prohibition, the Cuban embargo, industry consolidation, and changing consumer tastes by mixing American manufacturing heritage with elite Dominican partnerships. In <strong>1986</strong>, fate linked the Newmans with the Fuentes in Tampa; Tabacalera A. Fuente soon rolled La Unica for Newman, and the relationship grew into one of the most durable brand-plus-factory alliances in premium cigars.</p>\n<p>Newman's Dominican ultra-premium marques, including flagship <a href=\"/node/diamondcrown/\">Diamond Crown</a>, MAXIMUS, Julius Caeser, and Black Diamond, are handmade at <a href=\"/node/tabafuente/\">Tabacalera A. Fuente</a>. Separately, <a href=\"/node/brickhouse/\">Brick House</a> and other Nicaraguan volume roll at the family's PENSA factory in Estelí. The company also operates further Nicaraguan capacity (including the PENSA factory story for brands such as Brick House, Perla del Mar, El Baton, and Quorum), so not every Newman brand shares the Fuente floor. The families also co-founded the Cigar Family Charitable Foundation, tying commercial partnership to long-running community work in the Dominican Republic.</p>\n<p>Map J.C. Newman as a U.S. family company with two manufacturing poles: Fuente Dominican ultra-premium and Nicaraguan volume/value brands. Cross-link to Arturo Fuente, Tabacalera A. Fuente, and Diamond Crown for the partnership that shoppers mean when they say \"Newman and Fuente.\" For organic search, ownership continuity (Newman family), Tampa headquarters, and 1895 founding are high-intent facts that retailer pages often omit.</p>",
  "diamondcrown": "<p><strong>Diamond Crown</strong> is the ultra-premium flagship line of <a href=\"/node/jcnewman/\">J.C. Newman</a>, handmade at <a href=\"/node/tabafuente/\">Tabacalera A. Fuente</a> in the Dominican Republic. Created for Newman's <strong>100th anniversary in 1995</strong> by Stanford Newman with Carlos Fuente Jr., the original Diamond Crown Classic pursued \"the best cigar in the world\" with multi-year aged Dominican binder and filler under Connecticut Shade or Connecticut Broadleaf wrappers, tissue-wrapped presentation, and packaging as ornate as top-shelf spirits. The 54-ring formats helped popularize thicker vitolas in the mid-1990s boom. Later extensions (Maximus-related programs, special finishes, and anniversary humidors) keep the brand at the top of Newman's Dominican ladder.</p>\n<p>Recent projects such as Diamond Crown Tampa push the partnership further, including Florida sun-grown tobacco grown with retail partners and still rolled by Fuente, proof that the Newman-Fuente relationship keeps innovating rather than freezing in 1995 amber. Construction, aging discipline, and limited positioning place Diamond Crown in the same ultra-premium conversation as <a href=\"/node/ashton/\">Ashton</a>'s upper lines and <a href=\"/node/opusx/\">OpusX</a>, though each marca has a different ownership story.</p>\n<p>On the map, Diamond Crown is a Newman brand manufactured at Tabacalera A. Fuente, not a Fuente-owned trademark. That distinction matters for collectors tracing who owns the name versus who rolls the cigar. Pair this node with J.C. Newman for company history and Arturo Fuente / Tabafuente for the factory and agricultural context behind the smoke.</p>",
  "espinosahabano": "<p><strong>Espinosa Habano</strong> is a core line from <a href=\"/node/espinosa/\">Espinosa Premium Cigars</a>, the company led by <a href=\"/node/erikespinosa/\">Erik Espinosa</a>. It was among the first house lines made at Espinosa's own <a href=\"/node/lazona/\">La Zona</a> factory in Estelí, which opened around 2012. The original version was a Nicaraguan puro.</p>\n<p>In October 2015 Espinosa relaunched the line with new packaging and a revised blend: an Ecuadorian Habano wrapper over Nicaraguan binder and filler, still rolled at La Zona. A 7 1/2 x 38 Espinosa Habano Lancero followed in 2019 with the same wrapper and Nicaraguan binder and filler.</p>\n<p>When <a href=\"/node/sixzeroone/\">601</a> and <a href=\"/node/murcielago/\">Murciélago</a> moved to <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernandez</a>'s San Lotano factory at the end of 2016, Espinosa Habano stayed home. Espinosa later told Cigar Aficionado that La Zona makes Espinosa Habano, <a href=\"/node/crema/\">Crema</a>, and <a href=\"/node/laranja/\">Laranja Reserva</a>, which makes the Habano one of the clearest examples of what Espinosa's own factory produces.</p>",
  "crema": "<p><strong>Espinosa Crema</strong> is the first Connecticut-wrapped cigar to carry the Espinosa name. Announced in 2016 by <a href=\"/node/espinosa/\">Espinosa Premium Cigars</a>, it uses an Ecuadorian Connecticut-seed wrapper over Nicaraguan binder and filler. La Zona operations manager Hector Alfonso described it as a \"gateway cigar\" for smokers new to the brand.</p>\n<p>Crema is rolled at Espinosa's own <a href=\"/node/lazona/\">La Zona</a> factory in Estelí, which <a href=\"/node/erikespinosa/\">Erik Espinosa</a> has said still makes Crema, <a href=\"/node/espinosahabano/\">Espinosa Habano</a>, and <a href=\"/node/laranja/\">Laranja Reserva</a> after <a href=\"/node/sixzeroone/\">601</a> and Murciélago moved to A.J. Fernandez. It is a separate blend from the value-priced La Zona Connecticut, and the range has grown from three sizes to include a corona, rabito, lancero, and box-pressed toro.</p>",
  "sobremesa": "<p><strong>Sobremesa</strong> is the flagship of <a href=\"/node/dunbarton/\">Dunbarton Tobacco &amp; Trust</a> and the first cigar <a href=\"/node/stevesaka/\">Steve Saka</a> released after leaving <a href=\"/node/drewestate/\">Drew Estate</a>. It debuted at the 2015 IPCPR show. The original blend uses five fillers: Condega C-SG seco, Pueblo Nuevo criollo viso, La Joya Estelí C-98 viso, an Estelí hybrid ligero, and Pennsylvania broadleaf ligero from Lancaster County.</p>\n<p>Sobremesa is made at <a href=\"/node/joyafactory/\">Joya de Nicaragua</a> in Estelí, the historic factory behind <a href=\"/node/joya/\">Joya de Nicaragua</a>'s own brand. In 2019 Dunbarton added <strong>Sobremesa Brûlée</strong>, a milder take that drops the Pennsylvania ligero, adds more Condega seco, and wears an Ecuadorian Connecticut Shade wrapper.</p>",
  "belladamacigars": "<p><strong>Bella Dama Cigars</strong> is a poker-themed boutique brand from St. Petersburg, Florida, founded by <a href=\"/node/chantelleavitt/\">Chantel Leavitt</a>. Lines carry card-table names: The King, The Queen, Ace, Jack, The Royal, Flush, and Full House, plus the lower-priced Dealer Select range.</p>\n<p>Leavitt started with a U.S. manufacturer and, after sampling with several factories in 2023, chose <a href=\"/node/tabacaleraaragon/\">Tabacalera Aragon</a> in Estelí. In August 2024 Bella Dama announced Aragon as the sole producer of all its cigars, relaunching on September 1, 2024 with upgraded wrappers, binders, and fillers and the new Dealer Select line. That makes Bella Dama one of many Estelí-made independents, the same production country as boutiques such as <a href=\"/node/definition/\">Definition</a>, without owning a factory.</p>",
  "karenberger": "<p><strong>Karen Berger Cigars</strong> is the Estelí cigar company led by <a href=\"/node/karenbergerperson/\">Karen Berger</a>, widow of Enrique \"Don Kiki\" Berger, a Cuban Crafters veteran and one of the first Americans to make cigars in Estelí. She launched K by Karen Berger at the 2016 IPCPR show, two years after his death, and in 2023 the company changed its name from K by Karen Berger to Karen Berger Cigars. The logo, packaging, and line names stayed the same.</p>\n<p>The cigars are made at the family's own <a href=\"/node/tabacaleraesteli/\">Tabacalera Estelí</a>, which Don Kiki founded in the 1990s. The portfolio includes the Don Kiki color labels and K by Karen Berger. One exception was the 2022 K by Karen Berger 25th Anniversary, made by <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernandez</a> to mark more than 25 years of the Bergers in the business.</p>",
  "domain": "<p>Domain Cigars is a vertically integrated boutique brand co-founded by <a href=\"/node/estebandisla/\">Esteban Disla</a> and <a href=\"/node/daniellance/\">Daniel Lance</a>. Disla chose Lance as partner, their collaboration began with a phone call, and together they built a \"soil to smoke\" operation linking Disla-family tobacco farms in Valle Regadío to rolling at <a href=\"/node/familiadisla/\">Tabacalera Familia Disla S.A.</a> in Estelí. Launched in 2024, the portfolio spans Neutron (Mexican San Andrés), Negentropy (Ecuadorian habano), and Entropy, built around the family's Despanic fermentation process and a third-wave Nicaraguan cigar-making philosophy Domain tells in its Few Masters documentary.</p>\n<p>Disla's four-decade career includes more than ten years as master blender at <a href=\"/node/nicasueno/\">Fábrica de Tabacos Nica Sueño</a> (home to RoMa Craft Tobac) and earlier work at <a href=\"/node/stgesteli/\">STG Estelí</a>, where he created blends for <a href=\"/node/cao/\">CAO</a>, <a href=\"/node/alecbradley/\">Alec Bradley</a>, and roughly thirty other companies. Lance brought nearly a decade of U.S. retail experience, co-founded Lujo Concepts (BTX cutter), and moved to Estelí years earlier to study tobacco, not initially intending to launch a brand. Domain's owner-operator model places it alongside <a href=\"/node/karenberger/\">Karen Berger</a> and <a href=\"/node/hvc/\">HVC</a> in the boutique-to-vertical tier.</p>",
  "elseptimo": "<p><strong>El Septimo</strong> is an ultra-premium Costa Rican brand founded in 2005 by French distributor Gilles Botquin and first produced at Pure Aroma Cigars S.A. in Costa Rica. In March 2019 its assets were bought by <a href=\"/node/zayayounan/\">Zaya Younan</a>'s Younan Collection, which renamed the business El Septimo Geneva SA.</p>\n<p>The portfolio is built around themed collections with luxury packaging aimed at collectors. Gilgamesh, launched in 2020, was the company's first line in a 50 ring gauge, aimed at U.S. smokers. Sacred Arts followed in 2021 with seven cigars, and the Emperor Collection arrived in October 2022 with ten. All are made at El Septimo's Costa Rica factory, which the company describes as handling farming, blending, aging, and rolling in-house.</p>\n<p>Younan holds the brand through the Younan Collection. The company's own announcement presented Gilgamesh as a response to U.S. demand for larger ring gauges, since every earlier El Septimo had been thinner than 50.</p>\n<p>Costa Rica's other collector-focused name on this map is <a href=\"/node/atabey/\">Atabey</a>, made by <a href=\"/node/selectedtobacco/\">Selected Tobacco</a> at <a href=\"/node/costaricatabacos/\">Tabacos de Costa Rica</a> in Santiago de Puriscal.</p>",
  "laaurora": "<p><strong>La Aurora</strong> dates to 1903, when 18-year-old Eduardo León Jimenes opened a small factory in Guazumal, near Santiago. That makes it the Dominican Republic's oldest premium cigar maker. In 1912 he and his brother Herminio moved it to Calle Independencia in Santiago, where it took the lion emblem still on its bands. Guillermo León bought La Aurora in 2011, separating it from the León Jimenes group.</p>\n<p>The cigars are made at the <a href=\"/node/laaurorafactory/\">La Aurora Cigar Factory</a> in Santiago. Lines include Preferidos, the 1495 Series, ADN Dominicano, Cameroon 1903, and 107. The factory also rolls for outside brands such as <a href=\"/node/fratello/\">Fratello</a>, and La Aurora sits in ProCigar alongside <a href=\"/node/lfd/\">La Flor Dominicana</a> and <a href=\"/node/pdr/\">PDR</a>.</p>",
  "definition": "<p><strong>Definition Cigars</strong> is a Dallas-based boutique brand that <strong>Jamond Hackley</strong> launched in <strong>2019</strong>. Early releases included Conception (January 2020, named for the Ometepe region), The Equalizer (summer 2020), the 919 (blended for a September 19, 2020 celebration and released in 2021), Walking Stick (a lancero, January 2021), Pig, and Noir Cameroon. On shelves the brand is easy to spot: most releases wear fabric rope bands in bright colors instead of paper bands.</p>\n<p>The best-documented production tie is <a href=\"/node/lucianopichardo/\">Luciano Tabacos S.A.</a> in Estelí. Definition's GEN413 (first called Genesis) was made with Luciano Meirelles, Luciano's CEO and co-founder, and launched at the 2023 PCA show as a 5½ x 54 super robusto. It uses an Ecuadorian broadleaf hybrid wrapper, a Mexican San Andrés binder, and Nicaraguan fillers from Condega, Ometepe, Pueblo Nuevo, and Chincagre leaf from Masatepe. A 7 x 42 lancero and a 6 x 52 toro joined the line in 2024. A GEN413 Maduro, which swaps the wrapper and binder, followed in 2025.</p>\n<p>Newer lines come from a factory the company has not disclosed, and all use Nicaraguan filler. Anillo (2025, 6¼ x 54) is the brand's first cigar with a Pennsylvania broadleaf binder. Pi5ot and One13 (2026, both 6 x 52) use San Andrés wrappers. Pi5ot is a basketball tribute to Jalen Rose and Slippery Rock, and One13 is named for the address of Hackley's grandmother's house. On Cigar Nexus, Definition maps to Luciano for GEN413 only. Treat other lines as made in Nicaragua at an undisclosed factory.</p>",
  "lure": "<p><strong>Lure Cigars</strong> was created by Lou Cross III of Tallahassee to honor his late father, Lou Cross Jr., a lifelong fisherman and cigar smoker who died in June 2023 shortly after turning 90. The brand pairs \"Tackle & Tobacco\" with the tagline \"Smoke Great Cigars & Tell Tall Tales,\" and soft-launched in September 2023 in ten-count bundles sealed in wax with Boveda packs.</p>\n<p>The first blend, <strong>Corojo Oscuro</strong>, is a Nicaraguan puro: double-fermented Corojo Oscuro wrapper, Estelí binder, and Estelí, Jalapa, and Ometepe filler, in fishing-camp sizes such as The Big Lou, The Lil' Tony, and The Lunker. It was first made at <a href=\"/node/tabacalerapages/\">Tabacalera Pages de Nicaragua</a>, a factory Cross met through his distributor, Valacari Cigars. A darker Oscuro line followed, along with the Connecticut broadleaf \"Last Cast,\" whose band carries 1933 and 2023 for Lou Jr.</p>\n<p>In fall 2025 Lure and Valacari moved production to <a href=\"/node/latabacaria/\">La Tabacaria</a> in Estelí. The limited <strong>Catch &amp; Release</strong> series tests new sizes and blends; its 2026 Slim Jim lonsdale (6½ x 42, Connecticut broadleaf) was capped at 1,800 cigars.</p>",
  "gurkha": "<p><strong>Gurkha</strong> is one of the most widely distributed premium brands, known for ornate packaging and a very large catalog across price tiers. Its owner, Kaizad Hansotia, was selling watches and duty-free goods when, on a 1989 vacation in Goa, India, he bought a local vendor's cigars and the Gurkha name for about $149 (some accounts say $143, in 1988). He first gave the cigars to clients as gifts, then built a business around them as demand grew.</p>\n<p>Gurkha doesn't own a factory and works as a negociant, matching lines to partner factories in the Dominican Republic and Nicaragua. Production has included <a href=\"/node/williamventura/\">Tabacalera William Ventura</a> and <a href=\"/node/lacarrillo/\">Tabacalera La Alianza</a> (<a href=\"/node/ernestoperezcarrillo/\">Ernesto Perez-Carrillo</a>'s factory) in the Dominican Republic, and <a href=\"/node/pdr/\">PDR</a>, which made the Gurkha 125th. In Estelí, <a href=\"/node/caoamerican/\">American Caribbean</a> has made Cellar Reserve Platinum (with filler aged up to 12 years), Heritage Collection, and Royal Challenge. More recently Colección Especial moved from PDR to <a href=\"/node/lapalma/\">Tabacalera Palma</a> in Tamboril.</p>",
  "lfd": "<p><strong>La Flor Dominicana (LFD)</strong> is Litto Gomez and Ines Lorenzo-Gomez's vertically integrated Dominican boutique, founded in the mid-1990s and built into a power brand around estate leaf, the Tamboril factory, and formats nobody else owned first. What began as Los Libertadores energy evolved into Double Ligero, Ligero, Cameroon Cabinet, Air Bender, and the patented <strong>Chisel</strong>, a box-pressed vitola shape that helped LFD stand apart on every shelf. The house made \"Dominican\" mean strength and invention, not only mild Connecticut.</p>\n<p>Production centers on <a href=\"/node/lfdfactory/\">Tabacalera La Flor S.A.</a> in Tamboril after earlier Santiago chapters, with farms such as La Canela feeding wrapper, binder, and filler ambitions rare among DR boutiques. Gomez, a former jeweler, built agricultural control and factory discipline into the brand's personality. Three decades on, LFD still pushes unconventional formats and full-bodied blends while earning top critical attention, sitting beside <a href=\"/node/arturo/\">Arturo Fuente</a> and <a href=\"/node/casacarrillo/\">Casa Carrillo</a> as proof that Dominican family factories still define boutique identity from soil to box.</p>\n<p>Map LFD to <a href=\"/node/littogomez/\">Litto Gomez</a> and Tabacalera La Flor. SEO: \"LFD Chisel,\" \"La Flor Dominicana Double Ligero,\" \"Litto Gomez factory.\"</p>",
  "ligaprivada": "<p><strong>Liga Privada</strong> is <a href=\"/node/drewestate/\">Drew Estate</a>'s ultra-premium traditional line (No. 9, T52, H99, and the Unico series of limited shapes), hand-rolled in a dedicated culture inside <a href=\"/node/lagranfabrica/\">La Gran Fábrica</a> in Estelí. Built on Connecticut River Valley broadleaf and long-aged proprietary tobaccos, Liga is full, dark, and slow, the house's answer to anyone who thinks Drew Estate only means Acid. Critical culture and collector demand treat core Ligas as modern classics of the Nicaraguan boom.</p>\n<p>As a marca under Drew Estate / <a href=\"/node/swisher/\">Swisher</a> ownership, Liga Privada still markets scarcity, blender seriousness, and separate production discipline from the infused portfolio. Unico releases keep the line event-driven without abandoning the core No. 9 / T52 identity that made the brand famous. Map Liga as a flagship house line of Drew Estate, not an independent boutique owner.</p>\n<p>SEO: \"Liga Privada No. 9,\" \"Liga Privada T52,\" \"Liga Privada H99,\" \"Liga Privada factory.\" Cross-link Drew Estate, La Gran Fábrica, and Jonathan Drew.</p>",
  "caldwell": "<p>Caldwell Cigar Co. was started in 2013 by Robert Caldwell and a circle of friends who set out to do everything differently, a brainchild built on the founder's earlier ventures, including Wynwood Cigar Factory in Miami and The Hotel Humidor Company, a hospitality-centric cigar operation. Early industry confusion over the brand's packaging and purpose was, in Caldwell's telling, proof they were on the right track: unconventional presentation paired with proprietary, rarely available tobaccos rather than run-of-the-mill leaf everyone else uses.</p>\n<p>The company's stated mission is deliberately modest: a friends-run outfit that never chased scale, focused on making great cigars in exceptional packaging while doing good and having fun. That ethos extends beyond the humidor, Caldwell has backed art initiatives and charitable work through Lost &amp; Found cigars, with ongoing efforts around beach cleanups, homeless hunger outreach, and inner-city education. Core lines such as Blind Man's Bluff, Long Live the King, Lost &amp; Found, and Eastern Standard deliver the \"new experience\" the brand promised at launch, blends as distinctive as the boxes they ship in.</p>\n<p>On the manufacturing side, Caldwell operates as a modern negociant: matching each project to the right rolling floor rather than owning a single factory. Production flows through <a href=\"/node/pdr/\">PDR</a> in the Dominican Republic, <a href=\"/node/nicasueno/\">Fábrica de Tabacos Nica Sueño</a> in Estelí, <a href=\"/node/williamventura/\">Tabacalera William Ventura</a> in Santiago, and collaboration work at <a href=\"/node/lacarrillo/\">Tabacalera La Alianza (Casa Carrillo)</a>. That multi-factory pattern on the map is the point, serious boutique brands often span countries and partners behind one cohesive marque.</p>",
  "crownedheads": "<p><strong>Crowned Heads</strong> is the Nashville-rooted boutique house co-founded by <strong>Jon Huber</strong> after his years at Drew Estate, launched into the 2010s boom with storytelling bands and serious Nicaraguan blends. Flagship lines such as Jericho Hill, Four Kicks, La Imperiosa, Headley Grange, and Mule Kick built a reputation for full flavor, clean construction, and cultural branding that felt independent without being chaotic. The company became one of the defining \"indie\" success stories of the decade: limited enough to feel special, consistent enough for national retail.</p>\n<p>Core manufacture is at <a href=\"/node/myfather/\">My Father</a> in Estelí under the García family, the same factory graph as <a href=\"/node/tatuaje/\">Tatuaje</a>, <a href=\"/node/jassumkral/\">Jas Sum Kral</a>, and Ashton's Nicaraguan marques <a href=\"/node/laaromadecuba/\">La Aroma de Cuba</a> and <a href=\"/node/sancristobal/\">San Cristóbal</a>. Historical projects also touch <a href=\"/node/nacsa/\">NACSA</a> and Dominican capacity such as <a href=\"/node/lacarrillo/\">La Alianza / Casa Carrillo</a> for select SKUs, so the map shows a multi-floor boutique rather than a single locked address. That My Father center of gravity is the main ownership-adjacent fact shoppers miss.</p>\n<p>On Cigar Nexus, Crowned Heads sits in the Estelí boutique cluster as a contract-excellence brand: U.S. creative control, García factory execution. Cross-link to My Father, Tatuaje, and peer boutiques (Foundation, Warped, Viaje) when explaining how modern independents scale without owning every brick of a factory.</p>\n<p>Crowned Heads has also worked with <a href=\"/node/lucianopichardo/\">Tabacalera Pichardo</a> in Estelí, which produced Mil Dias. Tim Ozgener, a former CAO executive, is a partner, and Crowned Heads distributed his <a href=\"/node/ozgener/\">Ozgener Family Cigars</a> Bosphorus at launch.</p>",
  "southerndraw": "<p><strong>Southern Draw Cigars</strong> was founded in 2014 by husband and wife Robert and Sharon Holt and is run with family and close friends as a U.S. veteran-owned company. The core portfolio follows a botanical theme: Kudzu, Firethorn, Rose of Sharon, Jacob's Ladder, Cedrus, and the value-minded Quickdraw. Rose of Sharon is named for Sharon Holt, and Jacob's Ladder, wrapped in Pennsylvania broadleaf, nods to their son Ethan \"Jacob\" Holt.</p>\n<p>Production comes from <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernandez</a> in Estelí (<a href=\"/node/sanlotano/\">San Lotano</a>), which Southern Draw calls its exclusive factory partner. The first Kudzu was made there in 2014, and Rose of Sharon and Jacob's Ladder followed in 2016. That puts Southern Draw among the outside brands A.J. Fernandez rolls, alongside names such as <a href=\"/node/guayacan/\">Guayacan</a> and <a href=\"/node/rojas/\">Rojas</a>, rather than a factory owner.</p>\n<p>The tenth-anniversary <strong>Decennium</strong> (2024) was an exception: a 6 x 46 corona extra made at <a href=\"/node/eltitandebronze/\">El Titan de Bronze</a> in Miami's Little Havana, with a Brazilian Arapiraca wrapper, Dominican Criollo 98 binder, and Nicaraguan filler. Cigar Aficionado has scored Jacob's Ladder Brimstone 93, Kudzu 92, and Cedrus 91.</p>",
  "kristoff": "<p><strong>Kristoff</strong> was founded by <strong>Lissette Perez-Carrillo</strong>, daughter of master blender <a href=\"/node/ernestoperezcarrillo/\">Ernesto Perez-Carrillo</a>, giving the brand a direct bloodline to the family's <a href=\"/node/casacarrillo/\">Casa Carrillo</a> legacy while remaining a distinct commercial marca. Core lines such as Original Maduro, GC Signature, Shiro, and Kristania target consistent construction and accessible premium pricing.</p>\n<p>Kristoff's Dominican production partner has not been independently confirmed for this map, so no Dominican factory is linked here. That separation matters: family knowledge and branding on one side, independent manufacturing relationships on the other, even inside one bloodline.</p>\n<p>SEO: \"Kristoff cigars,\" \"Lissette Perez-Carrillo,\" \"Kristoff vs E.P. Carrillo.\" Cross-link Ernesto, Casa Carrillo, and William Ventura.</p>",
  "montecristonc": "<p><strong>Montecristo (Non-Cuban)</strong> is the U.S. and non-embargo expression of one of the world's most famous cigar names, entirely separate from Habanos S.A.'s Cuban Montecristo. In the United States the marca is controlled by <a href=\"/node/altadis/\">Altadis U.S.A.</a> (Imperial Brands / Tabacalera USA), <em>not</em> by <a href=\"/node/stg/\">STG</a> or <a href=\"/node/generalcigar/\">General Cigar</a>. That ownership fact is frequently confused online; STG holds other legacy names (Cohiba NC, Partagas NC, Punch NC), while Montecristo sits on the Imperial/Altadis side of the split with Romeo y Julieta and Trinidad.</p>\n<p>Non-Cuban Montecristo spans approachable Dominican core lines and ambitious Nicaraguan projects. Landmark collaborations include work with the <a href=\"/node/plasencia/\">Plasencia</a> family (Espada and related Nicaraguan puros) and high-scoring releases blended with <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernandez</a> at <a href=\"/node/sanlotano/\">San Lotano</a>, including the Montecristo 1935 Anniversary Nicaragua series that earned major <em>Cigar Aficionado</em> recognition. Product chief Rafael Nodal and Fernandez also partnered on related Altadis programs (Romeo San Andrés, later Trinidad Espiritu), making Montecristo part of a deliberate strategy: heritage trademark, Estelí fire, and modern packaging for U.S. retail.</p>\n<p>On Cigar Nexus, Montecristo (NC) is an ownership and production story. Altadis trademark control, multi-country manufacture (Dominican volume plus Nicaraguan prestige projects), and a hard line between this brand and the Cuban factory system that still defines the name outside the U.S. Use this node with <a href=\"/node/ryjnc/\">Romeo y Julieta (NC)</a> and <a href=\"/node/trinidadespiritu/\">Trinidad Espiritu</a> for the Altadis cluster, and with STG's Cohiba/Partagas pages when mapping the full Cuban-legacy trademark landscape.</p><p>Montecristo is among the most searched cigar names on earth, which makes ownership accuracy non-negotiable. A U.S. Montecristo is an Altadis/Imperial product, not an STG cigar and not a Habanos export. Cuban Montecristo never appears legally in the United States; the non-Cuban line fills that demand with Dominican volume SKUs and Nicaraguan prestige projects that have earned serious critic scores. Treat Espada, 1935 Anniversary, and core White/Classic lines as one trademark family with very different factory stories underneath.</p>",
  "ryjnc": "<p><strong>Romeo y Julieta (Non-Cuban)</strong> carries the 1875-era name into the modern U.S. market under <a href=\"/node/altadis/\">Altadis U.S.A.</a> (Imperial Brands / Tabacalera USA). It is parallel to, but legally and operationally separate from, Habanos S.A.'s Cuban Romeo y Julieta. Familiar U.S. lines such as <strong>1875</strong>, <strong>Reserva Real</strong>, Vintage, and related extensions emphasize approachable to medium-plus profiles for broad retail distribution: the everyday counterpart to Cuban Churchill mythology rather than a boutique Estelí cult brand.</p>\n<p>While much historical volume has been Dominican, Altadis has repeatedly tapped Nicaraguan capacity for prestige projects. This map records contract production ties to <a href=\"/node/plasencia/\">Plasencia</a>, whose multi-country factories support many outside brands. High-profile work with <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernandez</a> (including Romeo San Andrés) sits in the same Altadis strategy that produced Montecristo Nicaragua programs and <a href=\"/node/trinidadespiritu/\">Trinidad Espiritu</a> at <a href=\"/node/sanlotano/\">San Lotano</a>: corporate trademark owner, product leadership from Rafael Nodal, and Estelí blending talent for lines that need more power than classic Dominican mildness.</p>\n<p>For ownership mapping, Romeo y Julieta (NC) lives on the <strong>Altadis / Imperial</strong> side of the Cuban-legacy split, opposite STG/General/Forged portfolios for Cohiba, Partagas, and Punch. That is the graph's core teaching: same historic names, two conglomerates, two manufacturing webs, zero connection to Habanos production for U.S. legal product.</p><p>Romeo y Julieta 1875 remains a default recommendation in countless U.S. shops, so the brand carries continuous demand from new smokers and gift buyers. Stronger limiteds and San Andrés-era projects show Altadis using Nicaraguan partners when the catalog needs more than mild Dominican comfort. Cross-read this node with Montecristo (NC) and Trinidad Espiritu to see how one Imperial product organization manages multiple Cuban-legacy names without mixing their factories or trademark paths with STG's portfolio.</p>",
  "fratello": "<p><strong>Fratello Cigars</strong> was founded in <strong>2013</strong> by <strong>Omar de Frias</strong>, a former NASA financial analyst who left aerospace to build a boutique house. Lines such as Classico, Navetta, Bianco, and Arlequin target medium to full profiles with clean construction and approachable luxury packaging. The \"brother\" naming and founder story are central to brand identity.</p>\n<p>Production is multi-country: edges to <a href=\"/node/lazona/\">La Zona</a> (Espinosa) in Estelí and <a href=\"/node/laisla/\">Tabacalera La Isla</a> in the Dominican Republic show a strategy of matching blends to the right floor. SEO: \"Fratello cigars,\" \"Omar de Frias,\" \"Fratello Navetta.\"</p>\n<p>Several Fratello blends come from the <a href=\"/node/laaurorafactory/\">La Aurora factory</a> in Santiago, including Fratello Oro (2016) and the 2026 Fratello 1821 Dominican Republic puro.</p>",
  "padilla": "<p><strong>Padilla Cigars</strong> was founded in 2003 by Miami's Ernesto Padilla, son of the Cuban poet Heberto Padilla, and his brother Carlos. Ernesto created the brand to honor his late father, whose persecution under Castro became known as the Padilla Affair. Padilla has always been a contract brand, and its factory list traces several eras of boutique cigar-making.</p>\n<p>The early hits, including Padilla Miami, 1932, and 1948, were made with <a href=\"/node/pepin/\">Pepín García</a>. In 2008 Padilla opened his own small Little Havana factory, and lines also came from Honduras (Series '68) and Nicaragua. By 2011 the flagship lines were rolled at <a href=\"/node/raicescubanas/\">Raíces Cubanas</a> with Aganorsa tobacco, and other lines at Tabacalera Tropical, the <a href=\"/node/tabsa/\">TABSA</a> predecessor. In 2012 Padilla discontinued those lines and moved to <a href=\"/node/eltitandebronze/\">El Titan de Bronze</a> in Miami (Miami 8&amp;11 Maduro, Invictus) and to Oliva's <a href=\"/node/tabolisa/\">Tabolisa</a> in Estelí (Padilla Reserva).</p>",
  "ozgener": "<p><strong>Ozgener Family Cigars</strong> is the brand Tim Ozgener launched in 2022, his first blend in roughly 12 years. The name carries history: his father, Cano Ozgener, founded CAO International in Nashville in 1968 as a pipe business, and the family launched <a href=\"/node/cao/\">CAO</a> cigars in 1995 before selling the company to Henri Wintermans in 2007. Tim left after CAO moved to Richmond, Virginia, in 2010, following the Swedish Match and Scandinavian Tobacco merger that created today's <a href=\"/node/stg/\">Scandinavian Tobacco Group</a>.</p>\n<p>The debut line, <strong>Bosphorus</strong>, was first rolled at <a href=\"/node/lucianopichardo/\">Tabacalera Pichardo</a> in Estelí. In late 2022 production moved to Tabacalera La Alianza, the Dominican factory of <a href=\"/node/ernestoperezcarrillo/\">Ernesto Perez-Carrillo</a> (<a href=\"/node/lacarrillo/\">Casa Carrillo / La Alianza</a>), and the wrapper changed from Ecuador Sumatra to Ecuador Habano 2000 while the filler stayed Nicaraguan. <strong>Aramas</strong> (2023) is also made at La Alianza, with a Mexican San Andrés wrapper, Ecuador Sumatra binder, and Dominican, Nicaraguan, and Connecticut broadleaf filler. The name joins Ararat and Masis, the Turkish and Armenian names for the same mountain, a nod to the family's Armenian and Turkish roots.</p>\n<p>Tim Ozgener is also a partner in Nashville's <a href=\"/node/crownedheads/\">Crowned Heads</a>, which shared trade-show booths with Ozgener and distributed Bosphorus at launch.</p>",
  "jassumkral": "<p><strong>Jas Sum Kral</strong> (\"I am the king\" in Macedonian) is the boutique brand of <strong>Riste Ristevski</strong> (also styled Riste Buc in earlier trade press), known for unapologetically full Nicaraguan blends and theatrical line names such as Red Knight, La Bomba, Fenix, and Kral. Since the mid-2010s it has operated as a small, personality-driven house rather than a corporate portfolio play.</p>\n<p>Factory history matters for the map. Early production was associated with Noel Rojas's <a href=\"/node/neworder/\">NOA</a> / Estelí collaborative factory era; by January 2018 Jas Sum Kral had moved production to <a href=\"/node/tabacaleraaragon/\">Tabacalera de Aragón</a> in Estelí. This map also links the brand to <a href=\"/node/myfather/\">My Father</a>, which has not been independently confirmed. That migration from NOA to My Father is a classic boutique lesson: blender brand stays constant, factory edges can change.</p>\n<p>SEO: \"Jas Sum Kral,\" \"JSK Red Knight,\" \"Jas Sum Kral My Father,\" \"Riste Ristevski cigars.\" Cross-link My Father and (historically) NOA/Rojas.</p>",
  "stolenthrone": "<p><strong>Stolen Throne Cigars</strong> is a Virginia-based boutique founded around <strong>2018</strong> by <strong>Lee Marsh</strong> and <strong>JR Cannon</strong>. The regal branding and \"claim your seat\" attitude sit on top of a deliberately simple catalog (a handful of core lines, usually two vitolas each) and a long production partnership with <a href=\"/node/noelrojas/\">Noel Rojas</a> at the <a href=\"/node/rojasfactory/\">Rojas / New Order</a> factory cluster in Estelí. Marsh has described finding Rojas through lounge networks and flying to meet him almost immediately, choosing Rojas because he offered full blending and leaf control rather than a take-it-or-leave-it house blend.</p>\n<p>Regular-production and limited lines (including releases such as Castle 919, named with personal history in brand storytelling, plus Habano and other wrapper projects) stay grounded in that Rojas relationship. Trade coverage at PCA and elsewhere continues to place Stolen Throne next to Rojas Cigars as a paired booth culture, not a random contract hop.</p>\n<p>SEO: \"Stolen Throne cigars,\" \"Stolen Throne Noel Rojas,\" \"Stolen Throne Castle 919,\" \"Stolen Throne factory.\" Cross-link Noel Rojas, Rojas factory, and NOA.</p>",
  "deadwood": "<p><strong>Deadwood Tobacco Co.</strong> is a <a href=\"/node/drewestate/\">Drew Estate</a> brand named for Deadwood, South Dakota, the Black Hills town known as a biker destination. Its first three cigars, Sweet Jane, Fat Bottom Betty, and Crazy Alice, were marketed together as the \"Yummy Bitches.\" Each is a Maduro-wrapped cigar blended with aromatic tobaccos that bring out natural sweetness, and each has its own \"vixen\" character on the band and box.</p>\n<p>Sweet Jane is a mellow 5 x 46 corona. Fat Bottom Betty, a medium-to-full robusto (5 x 54, with toro and gordito sizes), uses a Connecticut broadleaf Maduro wrapper over Nicaraguan binder and filler, with notes of wood, leather, pepper, and coffee. Crazy Alice is a 4 1/2 x 46 with chocolate and dried-fruit notes. Leather Rose, a full-bodied 5 x 54 torpedo, joined the family in 2020, and Baby Jane is a 4 x 32 cigarillo sold in tins.</p>\n<p>Deadwood is handmade in Estelí at <a href=\"/node/lagranfabrica/\">La Gran Fábrica Drew Estate</a>, the same factory behind <a href=\"/node/acid/\">Acid</a>, <a href=\"/node/ligaprivada/\">Liga Privada</a>, and <a href=\"/node/undercrown/\">Undercrown</a>. Drew Estate has been owned by <a href=\"/node/swisher/\">Swisher International</a> since 2014.</p>",
  "leafbyoscar": "<p><strong>Leaf by Oscar</strong> is the brand of Pittsburgh retailer <a href=\"/node/jimrobinson/\">\"Island Jim\" Robinson</a>, made by <a href=\"/node/oscarvalladares/\">Oscar Valladares</a> at his factory in Danlí, Honduras. Robinson sold Valladares's 2012 cigar in his Leaf &amp; Bean shop and asked for a private blend wrapped in a whole tobacco leaf, like the 2012. That leaf wrapping became the brand's signature look.</p>\n<p>The line comes in four wrappers (Honduran Connecticut, Nicaraguan Jalapa maduro, Honduran corojo, and Ecuador Sumatra) over Honduran binder and filler. Since 2017 Robinson and Valladares's OV Cigars have jointly handled distribution. The 2025 Broadleaf Limited Edition was the first Valladares cigar with a Connecticut broadleaf wrapper. Robinson's sister brand is <a href=\"/node/islandjim/\">Island Jim</a>.</p>",
  "oscarvalladares": "<p><strong>Oscar Valladares Tobacco &amp; Co.</strong> is the Danlí, Honduras company of Oscar Valladares, who started out driving visitors around Honduras for <a href=\"/node/rockypatel/\">Rocky Patel</a>. Patel later gave him a job in quality control, and Valladares worked with the company until 2011. He started his own production in Danlí in 2012 with the 2012 by Oscar, a cigar wrapped in an unfermented leaf instead of cellophane, timed to the Mayan-calendar date of December 21, 2012.</p>\n<p>The 2012 led to his best-known partnership. Pittsburgh retailer <a href=\"/node/jimrobinson/\">\"Island Jim\" Robinson</a> sold it at his Leaf &amp; Bean shop and ordered a leaf-wrapped private blend. The first 1,000 cigars sold out in about a week and a half in 2013, and the line became <a href=\"/node/leafbyoscar/\">Leaf by Oscar</a>. The Danlí factory also makes Robinson's <a href=\"/node/islandjim/\">Island Jim</a>.</p>\n<p>House lines include The Oscar (2016), Super Fly, Rosalila (2013), and Altar Q. The last two are named for monuments at the Mayan site of Copán, where the company grows about 200 acres of tobacco, currently used as filler, and ferments leaf.</p>",
  "islandjim": "<p><strong>Island Jim</strong> is the Honduran brand named for <a href=\"/node/jimrobinson/\">\"Island Jim\" Robinson</a>, the retailer behind Leaf &amp; Bean. It was developed with <a href=\"/node/oscarvalladares/\">Oscar Valladares</a> and is rolled at his factory in Danlí. It grew out of the same partnership as <a href=\"/node/leafbyoscar/\">Leaf by Oscar</a>, which began when Robinson sold Valladares's 2012 cigar in his shop and ordered a leaf-wrapped private blend.</p>\n<p>The line includes Island Jim No. 2 and a Connecticut version, Connecticut #Two. The Connecticut #Two is a 6 1/2 x 52 torpedo in a Honduran Connecticut wrapper, made by Oscar Valladares Tobacco &amp; Co. It debuted at the 2023 Tobacco Plus Expo and sold for about $12 in 21-count boxes. A newer Leaf &amp; Bean by Island Jim shop, founded in 2024 in St. Augustine, Florida, carries the line.</p>\n<p>The 2025 Island Jim Barber Pole (6 x 52) twists Honduran Connecticut and Corojo wrappers together over an undisclosed blend. It debuted at the 2025 PCA show alongside the Leaf by Oscar Broadleaf Limited Edition, both made in Danlí.</p>",
  "lhpremium": "<p><strong>LH Premium Cigars</strong> (Lavida Habana) is the international boutique brand of Greek-American blender <a href=\"/node/nicholassyris/\">Nicholas Syris</a>. Core Claro, Colorado, Maduro, and Nick lines (including Nikos and the fuller San Andrés-wrapped Nicolas) are rolled at <a href=\"/node/costaricatabacos/\">Tabacos de Costa Rica</a>, giving LH a permanent Costa Rican production pin beside Lampert, Casdagli, and Selected Tobacco's ultra-luxury stack.</p>\n<p>Syris's story spans tobacco-family roots, U.S. entrepreneurship, and private-lounge culture abroad; the non-Cuban LH portfolio is the map-relevant commercial face. Collaboration <a href=\"/node/nickandjim/\">Nick &amp; Jim</a> with retailer <a href=\"/node/jimrobinson/\">Island Jim Robinson</a> also routes through the same Costa Rican factory, linking LH to the broader boutique graph without collapsing into Honduran Island Jim / Leaf by Oscar production.</p>\n<p>On Cigar Nexus, LH Premium is a Costa Rica boutique node with a strong person story. Cross-link Nicholas Syris, Nick &amp; Jim, and Tabacos de Costa Rica. SEO: \"LH Premium cigars,\" \"Lavida Habana cigars,\" \"Nicholas Syris.\"</p>",
  "nickandjim": "<p><strong>Nick &amp; Jim</strong> is the collaboration between <a href=\"/node/nicholassyris/\">Nicholas Syris</a> (LH Premium) and Pittsburgh retailer <a href=\"/node/jimrobinson/\">\"Island Jim\" Robinson</a>, rolled at <a href=\"/node/costaricatabacos/\">Tabacos de Costa Rica</a>. The partners met in cigar travel circles; Syris blended a house project that Robinson pushed into a branded release. Early naming as Lavida Isla shifted to Nick &amp; Jim (including P.B.E. pre-band editions) after commercial naming hurdles, keeping a multi-origin recipe in a 6x54-class toro story familiar to LH fans.</p>\n<p>On the map, Nick &amp; Jim is a distinct collaboration node inside LH's orbit, not a collapse of Robinson's Honduran <a href=\"/node/islandjim/\">Island Jim</a> / <a href=\"/node/leafbyoscar/\">Leaf by Oscar</a> factory edges into Costa Rica. Factory truth matters: this collab is Costa Rican production; Island Jim core lines remain on the Honduran Oscar Valladares graph.</p>\n<p>Cross-link LH Premium, Nicholas Syris, Island Jim, and Tabacos de Costa Rica. SEO: \"Nick and Jim cigars,\" \"Lavida Isla,\" \"Island Jim Nicholas Syris.\"</p>",
  "lampert": "<p><strong>Lampert Cigars</strong> is the brand of Dr. Stefan Lampert, a Liechtenstein-born attorney, launched in 2017 with a small-batch, bespoke approach. Its Costa Rican lines, the <strong>Oro</strong> line and <strong>Ocean Breeze</strong>, are handmade in Puriscal at <a href=\"/node/costaricatabacos/\">Tabacos de Costa Rica</a>, which also rolls <a href=\"/node/atabey/\">Atabey</a> and <a href=\"/node/byron/\">Byron</a> for <a href=\"/node/selectedtobacco/\">Selected Tobacco</a>, as well as <a href=\"/node/vegassantiago/\">Vegas de Santiago</a>.</p>\n<p>Ocean Breeze was updated in 2022 as a 5 x 54 robusto and later expanded with Gordito, Robusto Grande, and Toro Grande sizes. Lampert's portfolio also includes 1675 and 1593 Edición Oscura, and the brand produces some lines outside Costa Rica.</p>",
  "casdagli": "<p><strong>Casdagli</strong> is a Swiss family cigar house with roots in Cairo cotton trading and Geneva blending culture. The <strong>Villa Casdagli</strong> line, launched in the early 2020s and named for the family's historic Cairo villa, rolls at <a href=\"/node/costaricatabacos/\">Tabacos de Costa Rica</a>, where it has earned recognition among Costa Rican boutique productions. Vitolas include Robusto, Pigasus, Corona Gorda, Toro, and limited salomóns that signal classic European boutique taste rather than Estelí power marketing.</p>\n<p>Other Casdagli projects have used Dominican capacity, so this map highlights the verified Costa Rican Villa Casdagli address rather than implying every Casdagli cigar shares one factory. That multi-country pattern is common for Swiss-family brands chasing specific profiles per line.</p>\n<p>Cluster Casdagli with Lampert and LH Premium as non-Selected Tobacco clients of the Costa Rica factory, under the same roof as Atabey/Byron luxury. SEO: \"Villa Casdagli,\" \"Casdagli Pigasus,\" \"Casdagli Costa Rica.\"</p>",
  "vegassantiago": "<p><strong>Vegas de Santiago</strong> is a Costa Rican cigar brand from Santiago de Puriscal, about an hour from San José, where tobacco grows on volcanic mountain slopes at roughly 1,100 meters. Lines include El Magnifico \"Heritage\" (Costa Rican filler and binder under an Ecuadorian maduro wrapper), El Unico \"D8\" (Ecuadorian Habano 2000 wrapper), Don Luis Gold Label, El Mystico, El Puro, and El Primero. The brand credits Cuban master Luis Santana Lamas, a 25-year H. Upmann veteran, with shaping its manufacturing.</p>\n<p>According to its German importer, the cigars are rolled in Puriscal by <a href=\"/node/costaricatabacos/\">Tabacos de Costa Rica</a>, a family business dating to 1997. The brand was co-founded by Rudy Niehaus and the founder of Tabacos de Costa Rica, and Rudy's son Marc Niehaus now commissions production there. The same factory makes <a href=\"/node/atabey/\">Atabey</a> and <a href=\"/node/byron/\">Byron</a> for <a href=\"/node/selectedtobacco/\">Selected Tobacco</a>, along with boutiques such as <a href=\"/node/lampert/\">Lampert</a>.</p>",
  "atabey": "<p><strong>Atabey</strong> is one of the world's most allocated ultra-luxury cigar marques: long-aged, tiny-production releases from <a href=\"/node/selectedtobacco/\">Selected Tobacco S.A.</a> and master blender <a href=\"/node/nelsonalfonso/\">Nelson Alfonso</a>, rolled at <a href=\"/node/costaricatabacos/\">Tabacos de Costa Rica</a>. Named for the Taíno mother goddess, the brand is defined by multi-year aging, scarce vitolas (Ritos, Delirios, Brujos, Black series, and kin), and retailer allocations that make Costa Rica a serious luxury address rather than a footnote on a band.</p>\n<p>Atabey sits at the top of the same factory that also rolls <a href=\"/node/byron/\">Byron</a>, Bandolero, Alfonso, and boutique clients such as <a href=\"/node/lampert/\">Lampert</a> and <a href=\"/node/casdagli/\">Villa Casdagli</a>. That shared roof matters for map readers: one Costa Rican facility hosts both five-figure humidor culture and more approachable premium projects. Packaging and presentation match the scarcity (ornate boxes, ceremonial naming) without claiming Cuban Habanos production.</p>\n<p>On Cigar Nexus, Atabey is the flagship luxury node of the Costa Rica cluster. Cross-link Selected Tobacco, Nelson Alfonso, Tabacos de Costa Rica, and Byron. SEO queries \"Atabey Ritos,\" \"Atabey cigars,\" and \"most expensive Costa Rica cigars\" should resolve here with factory and ownership clarity.</p>",
  "latelier": "<p><strong>L'Atelier</strong> (French for \"the workshop\") is <a href=\"/node/petejohnson/\">Pete Johnson</a>'s second marca, launched in <strong>2012</strong> as L'Atelier Imports, a sister company to <a href=\"/node/tatuaje/\">Tatuaje</a> rather than a Tatuaje line extension. Johnson started it with his brother K.C. and friends including Dan Welsh (Surrogates). The flagship LAT sizes were a wink at Cohiba Behike vitolas, pigtail caps and all, but the blend is Nicaraguan, not Cuban. Cigar Aficionado put an L'Atelier in its Top 25 of 2013 (No. 12). Later pillars include La Mission, Selection Speciale, Côte d'Or, Maduro, and the Surrogates series.</p>\n<p>Almost everything is rolled at <a href=\"/node/myfather/\">My Father</a> in Estelí with the <a href=\"/node/pepin/\">García</a> family, the same factory that made Tatuaje famous. The L'Atelier signature (except Surrogates) is Sancti Spíritus, a finicky Criollo × Pelo de Oro hybrid grown in Ecuador and used as wrapper or in the blend. That leaf, plus wine-world naming, is how the workshop is supposed to feel different from tattoo-shop Tatuaje while sharing construction DNA.</p>\n<p>On Cigar Nexus, L'Atelier is Pete Johnson + My Father, not an Altadis or STG name. Map it next to Tatuaje. Cuban Behike comparisons are visual homage only.</p>",
  "hoyonc": "<p><strong>Hoyo de Monterrey (Non-Cuban)</strong> is the U.S. marca owned by <a href=\"/node/generalcigar/\">General Cigar</a> / <a href=\"/node/stg/\">Scandinavian Tobacco Group</a>, not Habanos S.A.'s Cuban Hoyo. After the Revolution, Fernando Palicio sold U.S. rights to Villazon (Dan Blumenthal and Frank Llaneza). Production moved to Honduras in the mid-1960s, among the first Cuban names rebuilt outside the island for American shops. General bought Villazon in <strong>1997</strong> and still holds Hoyo on the General side of the STG split (with Macanudo and CAO), while Punch, the old Villazon sibling, has been sold through Forged in recent shuffles.</p>\n<p>Honduras remains the brand's U.S. identity. Core and refreshed lines such as Excalibur, Hoyo de Tradición, Epicure Selección, Rojo, and Oscuro are tied to STG's <a href=\"/node/stgdanli/\">Danlí (HATSA)</a> factory. General has also used <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernandez</a> for Hoyo La Amistad Dark Sumatra, so the graph includes an Estelí collaboration edge without turning Hoyo into a Nicaraguan-only story. Cuban Hoyo (Epicure No. 2 lore, and the rest) does not appear on this map.</p>\n<p>On Cigar Nexus, Hoyo (NC) sits with Punch (NC) as the Villazon-into-General Honduran pair, opposite Altadis names (Montecristo, Romeo, H. Upmann) and still inside STG, not Imperial.</p>",
  "hupmannnc": "<p><strong>H. Upmann (Non-Cuban)</strong> is the U.S.-legal marca that shares a name with one of Cuba's oldest cigar houses (founded 1844 by German banker Hermann Upmann) and almost nothing else in production. Cuban H. Upmann remains a Habanos S.A. brand. The cigars sold in American shops are <a href=\"/node/altadis/\">Altadis U.S.A.</a> / Tabacalera USA products: Dominican volume lines plus Nicaraguan prestige projects. Confusing the two is the same trap as Montecristo or Romeo y Julieta. This node is the Altadis tree, not Havana.</p>\n<p>Core U.S. catalog names include Vintage Cameroon, The Banker, 1844 Reserve, Legacy, and related specials such as Herman's Batch (a 2019 Nodal / Tabacalera de García tribute to the founder). Historically those Dominican cigars came from the huge <a href=\"/node/tabdegarcia/\">Tabacalera de García</a> campus in La Romana, the same floor long used for non-Cuban Montecristo and Romeo y Julieta. Altadis has also built a parallel Nicaraguan chapter with <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernandez</a> at <a href=\"/node/sanlotano/\">San Lotano</a>: H. Upmann Nicaragua by AJ Fernandez (2017), Heritage, anniversary puros, and later AJ-stamped runs as Dominican supply was disrupted. <a href=\"/node/rafaelnodal/\">Rafael Nodal</a> is the product-leadership face of those modern blends.</p>\n<p>On Cigar Nexus, H. Upmann (NC) sits with Montecristo (NC) and Romeo y Julieta (NC) under Altadis, opposite STG's Cohiba/Partagas/Punch names. Map Tabacalera de García for the Dominican factory story and San Lotano for the AJ Nicaragua lines. Cuban H. Upmann does not appear on this graph.</p>",
  "agingroom": "<p><strong>Aging Room</strong> is the boutique marca created by <a href=\"/node/rafaelnodal/\">Rafael Nodal</a> and launched in <strong>2011</strong> under his Miami company <a href=\"/node/boutiqueblends/\">Boutique Blends</a> (the former Habana Cuba Cigar Co.). The idea was small-batch, extra-aged tobacco rather than a giant catalog: rare lots, limited runs, and blends named like a music program. It worked. Cigar Aficionado placed early Aging Room cigars in its Top 25, named the Quattro F55 Concerto No. 2 of 2013, and later named the Quattro Nicaragua Maestro No. 1 Cigar of the Year in 2019 (96 points). That last cigar is the one most smokers mean when they say Aging Room today.</p>\n<p>Ownership and distribution are easy to mix up. Nodal still owns Aging Room. <a href=\"/node/altadis/\">Altadis U.S.A.</a> has distributed the brand since <strong>2017</strong>, and Nodal is also Altadis vice president of product capability, blending heritage projects such as Montecristo Nicaragua and Trinidad Espiritu. The company is not an Altadis house marca the way Montecristo (NC) is. Production splits by line. Core Dominican Aging Room (Quattro Original, Small Batch M356, Solera, and related) is rolled with José \"Jochy\" Blanco at <a href=\"/node/lapalma/\">Tabacalera Palma</a> in Tamboril. The Nicaraguan flagship, Quattro Nicaragua, and the 2025 La Boheme reboot are made with <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernandez</a> at <a href=\"/node/sanlotano/\">San Lotano</a>. Cigar Aficionado has also noted some Aging Room work with Nestor Plasencia Jr., so the graph includes a lighter <a href=\"/node/plasenciaesteli/\">Plasencia Estelí</a> production edge.</p>\n<p>On Cigar Nexus, Aging Room is the boutique node that sits on both the Dominican Palma floor and the Estelí A.J. contract web, owned by Nodal, sold through Altadis. Map it when asking who makes Quattro Nicaragua, whether Aging Room is an Altadis brand, or how a blender can work inside a conglomerate without handing over the marca.</p>",
  "byron": "<p><strong>Byron</strong> is <a href=\"/node/selectedtobacco/\">Selected Tobacco</a>'s tribute to 19th-century Cuban cigar elegance: limited, extensively aged cigars blended by <a href=\"/node/nelsonalfonso/\">Nelson Alfonso</a> and produced at <a href=\"/node/costaricatabacos/\">Tabacos de Costa Rica</a>. Lines such as Poetry, Epicure, and related Edición programs chase classical vitolas, refined strength, and presentation that competes in the same ultra-premium conversation as sibling <a href=\"/node/atabey/\">Atabey</a>, with a slightly different aesthetic and naming language.</p>\n<p>Like Atabey, Byron is not a volume Dominican or Estelí catalog brand. Aging time and small batch counts define the business model. The Costa Rican factory address is deliberate: Alfonso and Selected Tobacco used quality and scarcity to put Puriscal-region production on the global luxury map, beside (not behind) Nicaragua's boutique boom.</p>\n<p>Map Byron with Atabey under Selected Tobacco and Tabacos de Costa Rica. Search \"Byron Poetry cigar\" and \"Byron vs Atabey\" need the shared blender/factory story and the ultra-luxury positioning made explicit.</p>",
  "mayflower": "<p><strong>Mayflower Cigars</strong> is the premium handmade brand launched in <strong>November 2023</strong> by <strong>Michael Knowles</strong>, host of <em>The Michael Knowles Show</em> at The Daily Wire. The name is personal history, not a marketing gimmick: four of Knowles's ancestors sailed on the Mayflower in 1620, and the brand presents itself as the culmination of a lifelong cigar habit that started with a family friend's machine-made Cuban, free sticks from rollers on Arthur Avenue in the Bronx, and a final Christmas gift of robustos from his mother. In Knowles's own telling, that same maternal gift box was made at the factory that now rolls Mayflower, <a href=\"/node/oliva/\">Oliva Cigar Co.</a>'s Estelí operation.</p>\n<p>Production is at <a href=\"/node/tabolisa/\">Tabolisa</a> in Estelí, the same floor behind <a href=\"/node/seriev/\">Oliva Serie V</a>, Melanio, <a href=\"/node/nub/\">NUB</a>, and <a href=\"/node/cain/\">Cain</a>. Knowles worked with Oliva designers on the blends; early trade coverage described the cigars as handmade in Knowles's preferred Nicaraguan factory, with logistics and DTC fulfillment through Brandshopper (parent of Cigarpage) and mayflowercigars.com. The core portfolio is built as a day-to-night arc: <strong>Dawn</strong> (mild to medium; Ecuador Connecticut wrapper, Cameroon binder, Nicaraguan long fillers), <strong>Dusk</strong> (medium to full; Ecuador Habano wrapper, Sumatra binder, Nicaraguan fillers), and <strong>Dream</strong> (double maduro; Pennsylvania Broadleaf wrapper, Mexican San Andrés binder, Nicaraguan filler, with notes the brand describes as chicory, cardamom, oak, and caramel). Limited <strong>Dawn of America</strong> (2026) reworks the Dawn recipe for the U.S. 250th anniversary with a seven-year-aged U.S. Connecticut shade wrapper over the same Cameroon/Nicaragua stack, sold in tubes of ten with a secondary band quoting the Constitution's \"to ourselves and our posterity.\"</p>\n<p>On the map, Mayflower is a clean contract-manufacturing story: a media-led brand identity, American heritage branding, and Estelí execution at industrial scale, without factory ownership. It sits on Oliva's Tabolisa node the way many outside projects do, but with an unusually public origin story (Daily Wire culture, college \"SIGAR\" club, cigar-review column, and the Pilgrim ancestry that named the marca). For enthusiasts tracing who actually rolls celebrity and media brands, Mayflower points straight to one of Nicaragua's largest vertically integrated floors rather than an anonymous Estelí address.</p>",
  "neworder": "<p><strong>New Order of the Ages (NOA)</strong> was the Estelí factory that <a href=\"/node/noelrojas/\">Noel Rojas</a> and Renegade Cigars owner Brandon Hayes opened on January 1, 2016, after Rojas split from the partners in his first factory, Tabacalera Aromas de Jalapa. The 5,000-square-foot factory made Rojas's own <a href=\"/node/guayacan/\">Guayacan</a> and <a href=\"/node/rojas/\">Rojas</a> lines, plus private-label and boutique work for other brands.</p>\n<p>NOA's outside clients included the original <a href=\"/node/jassumkral/\">Jas Sum Kral</a> releases, Xiphos, Ohana, Prendelo, and Kilo, a cigar made for The Cigar Authority. The arrangement didn't last. By January 2018 Jas Sum Kral had moved production to <a href=\"/node/tabacaleraaragon/\">Tabacalera de Aragón</a> in Estelí, and by mid-2018 NOA was reported closed while Rojas planned a factory in Texas.</p>\n<p>Rojas returned to Nicaragua in 2021 with a third factory, a converted Estelí movie theater now called the Rojas Cigar Factory (<a href=\"/node/rojasfactory/\">Rojas Cigar Factory</a>). An earlier version of this map said NOA cigars were rolled at A.J. Fernandez. We found no source for that, so the link has been removed.</p>",
  "acid": "<p><strong>Acid</strong> is the infused brand that made <a href=\"/node/drewestate/\">Drew Estate</a> famous. Jonathan Drew and Marvin Samel started the company in New York in the late 1990s. They named Acid after Brooklyn artist Scott \"Acid\" Chester (Arielle Chester Industrial Design). The cigars debuted around the 1999 trade show. Drew Estate calls them infused, not flavored: long-filler tobacco aged with herbs, oils, and botanicals in aroma rooms, not a cheap spray on short filler. The recipe is treated as a trade secret.</p>\n<p>Who makes Acid? <a href=\"/node/jonathandrew/\">Jonathan Drew</a>'s company, by hand at <a href=\"/node/lagranfabrica/\">La Gran Fábrica</a> in Estelí. Familiar lines include Kuba Kuba, Blondie, Cold Infusion, 1400cc, and Krush. <a href=\"/node/swisher/\">Swisher</a> bought Drew Estate in 2014, so Acid sits inside that company, but the factory and the infusion culture stayed in Estelí. Acid is the infused pole of Drew Estate. <a href=\"/node/ligaprivada/\">Liga Privada</a> is the traditional pole. Same campus, different idea.</p>",
  "diesel": "<p><strong>Diesel</strong> is a full-bodied catalog brand blended and rolled by <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernandez</a>, not one of his house marcas. Cigars International launched it in <strong>2009</strong> as a mail-order exclusive, in the same era as sister catalog brand <a href=\"/node/manowar/\">Man O' War</a>. The first vitola was a thick belicoso called the Unholy Cocktail. Unlimited followed in 2010. Later lines include Whiskey Row, Grind, and Sunday Gravy.</p>\n<p>Who makes Diesel? Tabacalera Fernandez at <a href=\"/node/sanlotano/\">San Lotano</a> in Estelí. A.J. blends it. Cigars International launched it, and <a href=\"/node/forged/\">Forged Cigar Co.</a> sells it in the U.S. now, inside <a href=\"/node/stg/\">Scandinavian Tobacco Group</a>. It is not San Lotano, New World, or Enclave. <a href=\"/node/manowar/\">Man O' War</a> is the sister catalog brand from the same factory, and that name stayed on the Cigars International side of STG.</p>",
  "undercrown": "<p><strong>Undercrown</strong> is <a href=\"/node/drewestate/\">Drew Estate</a>'s traditional maduro brand, and it started as the cigar the rollers smoked. Around 2009, <a href=\"/node/ligaprivada/\">Liga Privada</a> demand at <a href=\"/node/lagranfabrica/\">La Gran Fábrica</a> was outrunning supply, and the torcedores were asked to stop smoking Ligas on the floor. They built their own blend from other primings of the same rare tobaccos. Drew Estate released that factory smoke in <strong>2011</strong>. The original, now called Undercrown Maduro, uses a Mexican San Andrés wrapper, a Connecticut River Valley stalk-cut Habano binder, and Brazilian Mata Fina and Nicaraguan filler.</p>\n<p>Who makes Undercrown? Drew Estate, by hand at La Gran Fábrica in Estelí. <a href=\"/node/jonathandrew/\">Jonathan Drew</a>'s company put the rollers' blend on the market. <a href=\"/node/swisher/\">Swisher</a> has owned Drew Estate since 2014. Shade followed in 2015, blended by Willy Herrera: an Ecuador Connecticut wrapper over a Sumatra binder. Sun Grown arrived in 2017 with an Ecuador sun-grown Sumatra wrapper. Undercrown 10 marked the tenth year. Liga Privada is the scarce broadleaf line. Undercrown is the one the factory floor made for itself.</p>",
  "manowar": "<p><strong>Man O' War</strong> is a full-bodied catalog brand blended by <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernandez</a>, not one of his house marcas. Cigars International brought it out nationally in <strong>2009</strong>, the same year as sister brand <a href=\"/node/diesel/\">Diesel</a>. The original uses an Ecuador Habano wrapper over Nicaraguan binder and filler. Virtue followed in 2010 as the milder Ecuador Connecticut line. Ruination, also 2010, pushes ligero from Nicaragua and Honduras under an Ecuador Habano wrapper. Armada came in 2011 as a limited super-premium.</p>\n<p>Who makes Man O' War? Tabacalera Fernandez at <a href=\"/node/sanlotano/\">San Lotano</a> in Estelí. A.J. blends it. Cigars International sells it, and that retailer sits inside <a href=\"/node/stg/\">Scandinavian Tobacco Group</a>. It is not San Lotano, New World, or Enclave. Diesel is the sister catalog brand, now sold in the U.S. by <a href=\"/node/forged/\">Forged</a>. Man O' War stayed on the Cigars International side of the same parent.</p>",
  "seriev": "<p><strong>Serie V</strong> is the cigar that made <a href=\"/node/oliva/\">Oliva Cigar Co.</a> a critic's brand. The family introduced it in <strong>2006</strong>, and it had its trade-show debut in 2007. Rolled at <a href=\"/node/tabolisa/\">Tabolisa</a> in Estelí, it is a Nicaraguan ligero blend, with fermented Jalapa leaf under a Habano sun-grown wrapper. A Serie V torpedo posted a 94 in <em>Cigar Insider</em> in 2007.</p>\n<p>In 2012 Oliva added Serie V Melanio, named for patriarch Melanio Oliva, who started growing tobacco in Pinar del Río in 1886. Melanio uses an Ecuador Sumatra-seed wrapper over Nicaraguan binder and filler, a step smoother than the original. The Melanio Figurado was <em>Cigar Aficionado</em> Cigar of the Year in 2014, at 96 points. Melanio Maduro is the darker line. <a href=\"/node/nub/\">NUB</a> and <a href=\"/node/cain/\">Cain</a> are separate Oliva sub-brands on the same factory floor. Serie V is the flagship. <a href=\"/node/fredvandermarliere/\">Vandermarliere</a> has owned Oliva since 2016. The blend and the factory stayed in Estelí.</p>",
  "brickhouse": "<p><strong>Brick House</strong> is a <a href=\"/node/jcnewman/\">J.C. Newman</a> brand named for the brick house in Hungary where founder Julius Caesar Newman grew up, family upstairs and a tavern downstairs. He introduced the marca in <strong>1937</strong> as a Cuban-tobacco cigar. It faded after the embargo. Grandsons Eric and Bobby Newman brought it back in <strong>2009</strong> as a Nicaraguan cigar, and Cigar Aficionado named it Best Bargain of that year.</p>\n<p>Who makes Brick House? J.C. Newman, at its own PENSA factory (Puros de Estelí Nicaragua) in Estelí, which opened in 2011. Classic uses an Ecuador Havana wrapper over Nicaraguan binder and filler. Maduro uses Brazilian Arapiraca. Connecticut is the milder line. Diamond Crown, the family's ultra-premium, is a different story: those are handmade at <a href=\"/node/tabafuente/\">Tabacalera A. Fuente</a> in the Dominican Republic. Brick House is the Nicaraguan value brand. Diamond Crown is the Dominican Fuente partnership.</p>",
  "bolivar_nc": "<p><strong>Bolivar (Non-Cuban)</strong> is the U.S. Bolivar sold by <a href=\"/node/forged/\">Forged Cigar Co.</a>, inside <a href=\"/node/stg/\">Scandinavian Tobacco Group</a>. It is not the Cuban Bolivar of Habanos S.A. <a href=\"/node/generalcigar/\">General Cigar</a> has held the U.S. trademark for decades, in the same Cifuentes-name family as <a href=\"/node/partagas_nc/\">Partagas (Non-Cuban)</a>. The cigar on shelves now is Honduran. Forged has sold it since February 2021, and it stayed with Forged in the February 2026 portfolio shuffle.</p>\n<p>Who makes it? <a href=\"/node/stgdanli/\">STG Danlí (HATSA)</a> in Honduras, the same factory floor as much of non-Cuban <a href=\"/node/hoyonc/\">Hoyo de Monterrey</a>. The line people still buy is Cofradia. The natural uses an Ecuador Sumatra wrapper, a Connecticut binder, and Honduran and Nicaraguan filler. Cofradia Oscuro uses a Connecticut Broadleaf wrapper and an Ecuador Sumatra binder. General relaunched the marca in <strong>2005</strong> and credited Daniel Núñez, Benji Menendez, Estelo Padrón (brother of <a href=\"/node/joseopadron/\">José Orlando Padrón</a>, and longtime HATSA manager), and <a href=\"/node/ernestoperezcarrillo/\">Ernesto Perez-Carrillo</a>, who was still at General then. In late 2024 STG dropped the regular Bolivar line and kept Cofradia and Gran Republica.</p>",
};
